WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.690
- Welcome to the Lifespan podcast,

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:04.640
where we discuss the science of aging

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.903
and how to be healthier
at any stage of life.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:11.710
I'm David Sinclair.

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:14.700
I'm a professor of genetics
at Harvard Medical School

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.590
and co-director of the
Paul F. Glenn Center

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.970
for Biology of Aging Research.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:21.810
I'm joined by my lovely co-author

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.570
and cohost Matthew LaPlante.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:24.403
Welcome, Matt.

00:00:24.403 --> 00:00:26.370
- I love that you called me lovely.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:27.273
- I think you are lovely.

00:00:27.273 --> 00:00:28.640
- Oh, you're nice. You're nice.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.990
- You can be nice sometimes.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.520
Hey, today in our episode,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.640
we're going to be talking
about exercise and other...

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.250
what you call adversity mimetics.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.630
But before we get to that today,

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:43.587
right after we stopped taping
in the last episode you went,

00:00:43.587 --> 00:00:44.807
"Oh my God, iron!"

00:00:45.762 --> 00:00:48.040
and then we had this really
great conversation about iron.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.040
And so I wanted to bring us back there

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.480
before we could move forward today.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.600
Is that all right?

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.020
- That'd be great.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.170
So the last episode, if anybody missed it,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.890
was about the foods that
we can eat or not eat-

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:01.730
- Or not eat, right.
- To live a long time.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:03.960
And it was remiss of me not to bring up

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.854
a pernicious element called iron.

00:01:06.854 --> 00:01:08.180
- [chuckles] Pernicious.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.013
- Yeah.

00:01:10.214 --> 00:01:11.520
It's one of the elements

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.690
that is turning out to be
quite a dangerous element

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.430
to having too much of in your body.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.940
Increasingly we see that people
who have high levels of iron

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.870
have accelerated aging.

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:23.703
- Okay, yeah.

00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:25.496
But iron is essential, right?

00:01:25.496 --> 00:01:26.720
We all need iron.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:29.380
In fact, people supplement
with iron a lot of times.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:30.213
- Right.

00:01:30.213 --> 00:01:31.046
Well, we need iron.

00:01:31.046 --> 00:01:32.240
It's a major component of hemoglobin,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.760
which carries oxygen which we need.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.480
But the levels don't need to
be as high as we once thought.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:40.730
In fact, people who live a long time

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:43.330
and have these diets that are recommended,

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.520
I typically say they
have lower levels of iron

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:48.210
and lower levels of hemoglobin.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.210
And this is not necessarily a bad thing.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.550
Conversely, people who
have very high levels,

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:55.760
either genetically, hemochromatosis,

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.910
or take a supplement and end
up with high levels of iron,

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:02.710
might be predisposing
themselves to accelerated aging.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.160
- Okay, so what about just...

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.960
I mean, this plays into your idea

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.470
about why people should limit
their meat intake though;

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:11.740
because meat intake, meat is high in iron,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:12.630
especially red meats,

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:13.590
especially are higher in iron.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:14.980
- That's one aspect.

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.950
But typically if people
are taking multivitamin

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.750
with high levels of iron,

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:19.970
this could be a really bad thing.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.740
It'll accumulate in tissues.

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:24.650
And we know now that
particularly ferrous iron

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.290
leads to the production of free radicals

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.630
that damage tissues in
the body and, even worse,

00:02:29.630 --> 00:02:31.880
it leads to the accumulation
of these zombie cells

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:33.480
which we call senescent cells,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.460
and those drive the aging process,

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:36.630
in part, large part,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:40.150
because they leach these
inflammatory molecules

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.630
that cause other cells in the vicinity

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.830
to become inflamed, to age,

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.965
to senesce and even cause cancer.

00:02:46.965 --> 00:02:48.670
- Let's back up for a second here,

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:50.180
because I think we're going to talk more

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:52.860
about senescent cells in a coming episode.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.370
But we have mentioned
free radicals before,

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:58.970
and I think that's a word
that gets thrown around a lot.

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:01.800
And maybe not everybody
understands free radicals.

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:02.633
- Right.

00:03:02.633 --> 00:03:04.870
And we're not just talking about
people in Portland, Oregon.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:07.430
- Or Eugene, or Berkeley.
- Yeah.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.070
So these are free radicals that...

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.190
These have been around for
millions of years, of course,

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:15.483
and we've known about them
for at least the last 70.

00:03:17.261 --> 00:03:19.380
There are three main
types of free radicals.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.700
There's the superoxide molecule,

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:23.750
which is quite damaging

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.050
and can lead to what are called

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.140
advanced glycation end products

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:29.450
which are basically
proteins that are damaged.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.210
It's one of the reasons you want to fast

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.480
sometimes during the day

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.140
to get rid of these damaged proteins.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:37.730
There's a superoxide anion, it's called,

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:41.400
which creates most of the
oxidative stress in the body.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.240
And then there's a third
one, hydrogen peroxide,

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:46.650
which you can buy a bottle
of and dye your hair blonde

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:49.820
or orange as my hair
went during the pandemic.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.990
Those three things are
really bad for cells.

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:52.900
- You dyed your hair during the pandemic?

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:53.831
- I got bored.

00:03:53.831 --> 00:03:54.664
I just dyed it.

00:03:54.664 --> 00:03:55.800
- How did it look?

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.655
- Horrible.

00:03:56.655 --> 00:03:58.020
It really went orange.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.980
I've been dyeing it ever
since just to cover it up.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.280
But it's almost gone, thank goodness.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.230
But this is where iron comes in:

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.010
When you have a superoxide molecule,

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.740
it'll be turned into peroxide

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.710
by an enzyme called superoxide dismutase,

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.789
which is extremely important
to get rid of that superoxide

00:04:15.789 --> 00:04:17.700
which is going to damage the cell.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.000
But then you get hydrogen peroxide

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.610
and we know how bad that is.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:21.950
Look at my hair.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:23.290
You don't want that circulating.

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:24.380
So then what happens

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.670
is there's a molecule called glutathione

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.000
which will turn that into water.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.100
But if you have too much iron around,

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:33.500
there won't be enough
chance to detoxify it

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:37.140
and it'll be turned into
this hydroxyl radical.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.210
And that's a really damaging molecule

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.830
that goes on to damage your DNA, your RNA,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:45.170
lipids, proteins, and
drive aging and senescence.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:46.620
So you want to have iron;

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.310
enough not to be lethargic,

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.550
to have enough oxygen transported,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.520
but not super high levels

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.430
that'll cause accelerated aging.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.350
- And this is going to be
different for different people.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.230
10% of the population in the United States

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:01.310
has the a HFE gene,

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:04.790
which makes them more
susceptible to hemochromatosis,

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:06.780
which means they have to
be even more concerned.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.530
You can't know this without knowing

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:10.770
if you carry this gene though, right?

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:11.870
I mean-
- Right.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.180
You do need to measure things.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.000
And that's why I, myself,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.200
and I recommend people measure
their blood bio chemistry

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.260
and determine whether their iron,

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:22.440
whether it's free iron,
which is the worst type,

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.870
or even the bound iron are out of whack.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.460
And you can correct for that.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.640
In the case of a normal person,

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:30.460
I would say don't take her an
iron supplement necessarily,

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.730
although women may need it
certain times of the month.

00:05:33.730 --> 00:05:35.510
But people who have hemochromatosis,

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:37.830
they're particularly susceptible to aging.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.480
And the recommendation, typically,

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.146
of a doctor is donate blood if you can.

00:05:42.146 --> 00:05:42.979
- Okay.

00:05:42.979 --> 00:05:46.020
So just to go over what
we talked about last time

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.780
really quickly.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.280
You advocate for a plant-based diet.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:55.820
You advocate for low iron levels.

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:57.180
What am I missing?

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.410
- Well, we're going to
talk about that today.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.210
What type of exercise works best.

00:06:01.210 --> 00:06:02.767
- Oh, I was just talking
about how we should eat,

00:06:02.767 --> 00:06:03.654
but yeah.

00:06:03.654 --> 00:06:04.487
[Matt and David laugh]

00:06:04.487 --> 00:06:06.100
- Well, eat less often.

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:06.960
- Eat less often?
- Yeah.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.930
So I've gone from not eating breakfast

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.840
now to not eating lunch as well,

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.250
and just focus on dinner;

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.080
and giving my body a
rest from food overnight

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.770
and through the middle of the day

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.480
so that it turns on our
defensive genes against aging,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.440
those three buckets:

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.680
the mTOR, which senses
amino acids or lack thereof;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.990
the AMPK, which senses energy,
which is glucose mainly;

00:06:28.990 --> 00:06:31.060
and the sirtuins, which I work on,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.370
that sense all of those
things, including exercise.

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:36.270
And those three protective mechanisms,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.690
we can turn on by eating the right things,

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:40.680
eating less often and exercising.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:42.520
- And this leads in really well, actually,

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:43.930
to what we're talking about today,

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:45.390
because what we're talking about

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:49.530
is bringing your body into a
state of perceived adversity

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.420
as a way to turn on these genes.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.630
Before we can do that,

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.190
we've got some business to take care of.

