00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:34.810
- That's a good starting point.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:36.480
So in the 1980s,

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:38.180
it was all about just free radicals.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.950
We didn't know that aging
could actually be controlled

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:42.410
by any genes,

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.220
it was certainly not single genes.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:47.120
And Cynthia came along as
a young graduate student

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.360
and looked for worms
that would live longer.

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:52.200
And she mutated them and
found a strain of worm,

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:53.280
a mutant worm,

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:55.140
that was living twice as long;

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.590
so instead of living for 15 days, 30 days.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.500
And that was a remarkable finding

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.640
because when she dissected
the worms, bred them out,

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.570
found that it was due to
a single gene mutation.

00:11:06.570 --> 00:11:09.230
And when she cloned the gene
and found out where it was,

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:12.360
it was in a gene that sensed insulin,

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.020
it's the insulin receptor
gene called DAF-2.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:16.853
That was remarkable

00:11:16.853 --> 00:11:18.850
because it had nothing
to do with free radicals,

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.560
it had nothing to do with DNA damage,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:22.710
it was a signaling molecule.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.790
And that was the start of a revolution

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:27.400
in understanding how to control aging

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:31.360
by using genes and supplements and drugs

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:33.340
that turn on the body's defenses.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:36.560
- And this was the first
so-called longevity gene?

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:38.120
- Yeah, I would say that was the first.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:42.360
It came very close to the work
that we were doing at MIT.

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:46.390
We were maybe a year
later than her, but we-

00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:48.210
- You seem a little annoyed by that.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:49.060
- Nope, it's not much me.

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:52.170
I'm careful because my
supervisor Lenny Guarente

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.300
used to get really annoyed

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.890
when he was told that
he was slightly behind.

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:58.990
He thought his work was
being done at the same time.

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.650
So those two used to be the...

00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:05.900
I guess maybe still are at
the forefront of this field;

00:12:05.900 --> 00:12:08.520
they're certainly the
grandparents, if nothing else.

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:10.410
And they both showed...

00:12:10.410 --> 00:12:12.370
So Cynthia in worms and
then Lenny in yeast,

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:13.803
where I was at MIT,

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.860
made the finding that a
single gene alteration

00:12:18.860 --> 00:12:21.980
can have a huge impact on
an organism's lifespan.

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:22.980
- And these genes,

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.760
there's a few of them now
that we've identified.

00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:29.890
And all of them seem to work
on a very similar circuit

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.500
that all starts with adversity.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.333
- That's right.

00:12:32.333 --> 00:12:34.280
These signaling proteins,

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:36.600
like the DAF-2 one that Cynthia discovered

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.170
and the sirtuins that we work on,

00:12:39.170 --> 00:12:40.882
we didn't know what they
were doing at first.

00:12:40.882 --> 00:12:41.810
It was like, "This is crazy.

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.070
It's got nothing to do with
DNA repair and free radicals."

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.370
But then when we tracked what
they were actually doing,

00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:50.150
it turns out they're speaking
to the rest of the cell

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:53.380
to keep the cell healthy and alive.

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:54.970
So in the case of that worm,

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:56.740
those worms weren't just longer lived,

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:59.210
the program to survive was activated.

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:00.990
Those worms are stress resistant.

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.280
You can hit them with heat,
you can hit them with cold,

00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:08.480
you can starve them or just
put them in happy conditions,

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:09.910
they live longer.

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:13.060
The paradigm is that
normally a worm responds

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:13.930
to its what's in the environment;

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.590
if it's starved, it'll
hunker down, it'll survive.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.840
But here we had a worm that
was mimicking starvation

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:22.720
and was able to have the benefits

00:13:22.720 --> 00:13:24.853
without having to starve itself.

00:13:25.837 --> 00:13:29.580
- How do the genes, for
lack of a better word, know?

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:33.030
How do they know that the
conditions are adverse

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:34.453
and they need to turn on?

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.010
- Well, there are various sensors.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.540
And so that the three main
pathways that control this:

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:41.630
we've talked about mTOR,

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:45.310
which is sensing the levels
of amino acids in the cell.

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:47.220
Lower levels of certain amino acids,

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.900
branch chain amino acids,

00:13:48.900 --> 00:13:50.410
are good for the cell

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.960
because they downregulate mTOR activity.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.380
That's one way.

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:57.160
The AMPK senses energy, glucose;

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.880
and then the sirtuin sense NAD,

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:00.490
which is a molecule that goes up

00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:02.380
with exercise and hunger.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:04.000
Together, those are the sensors.

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.640
Now, how does a cell
communicate with other cells?

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:07.920
Well, there's insulin.

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:09.490
Like we know when we eat a meal,

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:12.560
insulin goes from our
pancreas into our bloodstream

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:13.887
and tells the rest of the body,

00:14:13.887 --> 00:14:17.920
"Hey, you got sugar already,
suck it in, use it."

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.330
That's a signaling protein.

00:14:19.330 --> 00:14:22.720
And what Cynthia's worms ended
up when they were mutated

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:27.210
is that that signal to bring
in the glucose was mutated

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:30.150
and this insulin signal was absent.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:33.070
And when don't have the
insulin signaling in your worm,

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:34.380
it means you're hungry

00:14:34.380 --> 00:14:35.820
and you get the benefits of being hungry

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.060
without having to starve.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:42.110
- So our body is this always
active monitoring system.

00:14:42.110 --> 00:14:44.060
We're perceiving the world around us

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:47.370
and the world around us
is constantly throwing

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.083
different stressors at us,

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:53.230
or at least it was in
human history, right?

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:57.070
We've always been a little
cold or a little hungry,

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:58.010
or a little scared,

00:14:58.010 --> 00:15:00.270
or having to run from
this animal or that animal

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:01.640
that wanted to eat us.

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:06.413
This is the environment
that our species evolved in,

00:15:06.413 --> 00:15:08.350
that all species evolved in, right,

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.660
is this constant state of adversity.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.493
- Right.

00:15:11.493 --> 00:15:14.130
And that adversity, we've
evolved to fight back.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:15.790
And so in the past,

00:15:15.790 --> 00:15:18.440
our bodies were constantly
fighting disease

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:20.040
and deterioration throughout life.

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:24.070
Even before birth, we're
fighting against entropy,

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:25.390
things are falling apart.

00:15:25.390 --> 00:15:27.530
The aging clock that we can now measure

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.300
begins at conception.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.700
But if you fast forward to now,

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:33.980
our society is built on comfort.

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:34.813
We've got...

00:15:34.813 --> 00:15:38.190
Most countries have
enough food and shelter.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:39.023
We can sit down,

00:15:39.023 --> 00:15:41.770
we don't have to run away
from predators very often.

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:44.710
And this is a real problem
because these longevity defenses

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:45.990
that normally would be activated

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:49.610
by being cold and hungry
and running are lethargic.

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:52.340
The body doesn't expend
energy to defend itself

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:54.210
unless there's a need for it.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.040
And modern society is the worst thing.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:58.350
- We've taken away those needs.

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:00.590
Yeah, we love comfort, it feels good,

00:16:00.590 --> 00:16:02.870
but that's the worst thing
for long-term health.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:04.600
We need to trick the body

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.500
into getting out of its comfort zone

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.720
by doing these things we've talked about:

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:09.940
eating the right foods,

00:16:09.940 --> 00:16:12.060
including foods that are stressed;

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:13.600
eating less often-

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:15.500
- Which puts stress on us,

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:17.940
which causes our body to feel like

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:19.160
it needs to protect itself.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:21.340
Because if we're not filling it

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:24.530
with all of the nutrients
and calories we want,

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:25.560
it perceives a need,

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:28.316
it proceeds adversity and
it turns on these genes.

00:16:28.316 --> 00:16:29.149
- Right.

00:16:29.149 --> 00:16:30.260
And as long as we have adequate nutrition.

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.100
That's what we call intermittent fasting

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.670
with adequate nutrition or IFAN,

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:37.290
this is another term we
just coined in this podcast,

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:39.730
that allows the body to
turn on these defenses

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:43.090
without suffering long-term
negative consequences.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.040
What's interesting, I find,

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:45.670
is that even though we know the fridge

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:47.730
is probably full with food,

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:49.430
our bodies are not that smart,

00:16:49.430 --> 00:16:51.280
our bodies don't get that message.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:52.590
And we can trick it,

00:16:52.590 --> 00:16:53.897
trick the body into thinking,

00:16:53.897 --> 00:16:55.150
"Oh my goodness,

00:16:55.150 --> 00:16:56.220
the fridge is running out of food,

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.170
or the field or the
forest is lacking in food,

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:01.740
we better get to hunker down and survive."

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:02.573
What are those things?

00:17:02.573 --> 00:17:05.200
You burn fat, you
increase your metabolism,

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:07.580
so you've got more energy
to run around and find food.

00:17:07.580 --> 00:17:09.100
You become more alert

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:11.060
because you've got to go find more food.

00:17:11.060 --> 00:17:13.950
And you defend your body against insults,

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:18.590
whether it's incoming infections
or diseases from within.

00:17:18.590 --> 00:17:20.340
And that's cool that we
can trick our bodies.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:21.720
And it's not that hard,

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.870
we just need to do the
things we're talking about

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:25.030
on this podcast.

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.920
- And when we're talking...

00:17:26.920 --> 00:17:29.780
We've already talked about the real,

00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:32.670
key effects that come
from eating less often

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:35.989
to basically turn on this
adversity mimetic effect.

00:17:35.989 --> 00:17:38.690
There's another really
easy way to do this though.

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:40.410
- Right, and that's get off your butt.

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:41.790
- Just get off your butt.

00:17:41.790 --> 00:17:43.420
- Yeah, It's not that hard,

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:46.190
as we say sitting here doing this podcast.

00:17:46.190 --> 00:17:49.180
But one way I do it is
I have a standing desk.

00:17:49.180 --> 00:17:51.930
so my butt actually atrophied.

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.390
When we were writing this book "Lifespan,"

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:57.289
I ended up with a cramp
in my piriformis muscle,

00:17:57.289 --> 00:17:58.210
which is the one that-

00:17:58.210 --> 00:17:59.260
- Wait, in your what?

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.650
- Yeah, yeah, it's a small muscle.

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.460
- Where is it?
- Size doesn't matter.

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:05.810
It's right in your hip,

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:08.040
it goes through that hole in your pelvis,

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:09.540
and it's essential for standing,

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:10.733
and it was cramped up-
- I always wondered

00:18:10.733 --> 00:18:13.140
what that hole was four.
- Yeah, it's-

00:18:13.140 --> 00:18:14.370
- There's a muscle that goes through it?

00:18:14.370 --> 00:18:15.250
- Yes.
- [Matthew] Okay.

00:18:15.250 --> 00:18:16.083
- All right?

00:18:16.083 --> 00:18:18.190
But it was cramped up
on my left-hand side.

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:20.640
So I was limping for about nine months

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.877
after we wrote the book, and I thought,

00:18:22.877 --> 00:18:24.930
"Great, I'm writing about
health and longevity

00:18:24.930 --> 00:18:27.210
and now I've crippled myself."

00:18:27.210 --> 00:18:28.620
I eventually got it to go away

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.760
with a combination of exercise,

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.970
physio and an injection of NAD in my butt,

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:37.050
which we'll get to in
probably the next episode.

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:40.340
But the point here is that
sitting down is bad for us.

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:41.650
You atrophy,

00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:44.076
you have less muscle which
means your hormone levels,

00:18:44.076 --> 00:18:45.830
particularly testosterone,

00:18:45.830 --> 00:18:46.920
will go down;

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:51.000
and you become in pain,
that's not a good thing.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:52.870
But ultimately if you
have not a lot of muscle

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:53.920
in your hips, particularly,

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:55.960
you can break your
bones when you fall over

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:57.330
when you're older.

00:18:57.330 --> 00:19:00.840
And all of that means you
need to get off your butt,

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:02.210
stand up;

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:04.220
even better, go for a walk; even better,

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:05.340
go for a run or cycle

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.470
to get what we call a hypoxic state going.

00:19:08.470 --> 00:19:10.830
Your body needs to suck in more oxygen.

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:12.950
And that has remarkable health benefits.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:14.580
- And we really have to work hard at this.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:15.413
'Cause, I mean,

00:19:15.413 --> 00:19:17.270
you're saying this as we're
sitting in these chairs, right?

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:19.830
A couple of days after I sat
down in a chair for a long time

00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:22.380
in an airplane to get here, right?

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:23.840
We've got a nice comfy couch over there

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.430
that we've been working on.

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:29.250
Like, we actually have to
actively pursue opportunities

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.240
to do this, to get exercise,

00:19:32.240 --> 00:19:35.170
to promote this adversity mimetic effect

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:37.040
because our lives are built

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:40.540
around comfort and sedentariness?

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:41.780
- Yes, that's true.

00:19:41.780 --> 00:19:43.400
And what I think most
people don't appreciate

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:46.010
is that exercise isn't just beneficial

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:49.570
for your fitness and for your vitality,

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.800
it actually can stop
diseases in their tracks.

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:54.360
Exercise can slow down cancer.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:58.230
In fact, it can prevent
up to 23% of all cancers

00:19:58.230 --> 00:19:59.780
from occurring.

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:01.300
That's true for cardiovascular disease.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.570
In fact, it has an even
bigger effect on that,

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:07.810
30% reduction just by doing
moderate exercise every week.

00:20:07.810 --> 00:20:10.170
50 minutes is sufficient,

00:20:10.170 --> 00:20:13.010
or three times a week with 10 minutes.

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:14.280
All-cause mortality, right?

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:15.113
So what we are...

00:20:15.113 --> 00:20:18.330
All-cause mortality is
basically slowing down aging,

00:20:18.330 --> 00:20:21.130
that's a 27% reduction
in the rate of aging

00:20:21.130 --> 00:20:22.130
just by exercising.

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:23.220
- The thing you just said...

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.670
I love this idea of connecting the idea

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:27.070
of all-cause mortality with aging;

00:20:27.070 --> 00:20:29.500
because as we've presented here,

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:32.083
as you've been arguing
for quite some time,

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:38.350
most diseases which bring us
toward the end of our lives

00:20:38.730 --> 00:20:43.100
are really just an accumulation
of the symptoms of aging.

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:46.660
And so if you bring down the disease rate

00:20:47.540 --> 00:20:48.730
across various diseases,

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:51.220
which is bringing down
all-cause mortality,

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:52.440
you're really just...

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:56.350
It's just another way of saying
we're bringing down aging.

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:57.277
- We must be. Exactly.

00:20:57.277 --> 00:20:59.970
And we can also now measure
that with the biological clock,

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:02.100
the so-called Horvath clock,

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.520
named after my good friend Steve Horvath.

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:07.070
That is now a measure of the process

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:10.710
that leads to all of these
diseases that kill us.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:12.860
And one way that we know for a fact

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:16.170
to slow down the ticking
of that biological clock

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:17.294
is exercise.

00:21:17.294 --> 00:21:19.803
- Any any kind of exercise is good.

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:25.690
This is why the 10,000
steps a day goal is popular;

00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:27.330
generally speaking, it's good.

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:29.330
It's not a magic number.

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:32.381
I think somebody came up with 10,000,

00:21:32.381 --> 00:21:36.380
and actually some studies
have shown health benefits

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:37.213
that are...

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:42.040
exist at 4 to 6,000, kind
of peter out from there.

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:43.700
But the basic idea here

00:21:43.700 --> 00:21:46.160
is that when we move we burn calories,

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:47.070
we're putting our bodies

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:51.350
into at least a low state of adversity.

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:52.879
- Right. So try to get 4,000.

00:21:52.879 --> 00:21:56.028
And 10,000 seems to be even better.

00:21:56.028 --> 00:21:57.080
- It's a great goal.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:21:58.440
It's a round number.

00:21:58.440 --> 00:21:59.273
It's fine, right?

00:21:59.273 --> 00:22:00.920
- Just get off your ass,
that's what we're saying,

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:01.753
that's all it takes.

00:22:01.753 --> 00:22:02.586
It's not that hard.

00:22:02.586 --> 00:22:04.020
Walk around the block a few times.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.790
And particularly after a meal,
as we spoke about last time;

00:22:06.790 --> 00:22:09.480
walking after a meal
gets your glucose levels

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:11.710
to be more level, which is what you want.

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:12.760
- Which relates to this...

00:22:12.760 --> 00:22:14.990
We were talking about this
old Chinese proverb, like,

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:16.620
99 steps after a meal...

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:19.620
No, a thousand steps after
a meal, you'll live to 99.

00:22:19.620 --> 00:22:22.100
But walk after you eat.
- Right

00:22:22.100 --> 00:22:23.460
- Because?

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.550
- Well, it'll stimulate
the uptake of glucose,

00:22:25.550 --> 00:22:27.770
but also it'll get your muscles moving

00:22:27.770 --> 00:22:29.900
and stimulate the production
of new blood vessels,

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:33.520
make sure that you don't run
out of oxygen later in life.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:35.260
But mostly what happens when you move,

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:36.830
the first thing that happens

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:38.200
is a really important reaction

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:40.120
that involves one of
these longevity pathways,

00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:43.010
the middle one that I
refer to, called AMPK.

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:48.010
- And we can turn on AMPK just
with a low level of exercise,

00:22:48.090 --> 00:22:50.190
just start that adversity?

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:53.180
- Yeah, so AMPK registers
energy in the cell,

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:56.310
which is chemical energy called ATP.

00:22:56.310 --> 00:22:58.710
And when you have low levels of ATP,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:00.480
AMPK gets activated;

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:03.430
and it stands for AMP activated kinase.

00:23:03.430 --> 00:23:05.940
And AMP is what you get a lot of

00:23:05.940 --> 00:23:09.190
when you don't have
enough of this fuel, ATP.

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.470
Long story short:

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:13.270
When you have high
levels of AMPK activity,

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:15.310
you will make more mitochondria

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:16.950
which gives you long lasting benefits.

00:23:16.950 --> 00:23:18.460
So after you've exercised,

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:20.480
your body will be making more power,

00:23:20.480 --> 00:23:24.260
more of these organelles, we call them,

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.530
that will actually give you
long-term health benefits

00:23:26.530 --> 00:23:28.890
beyond the period of exercise.

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:33.890
- We know that low level
exercise alone is good,

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:35.763
it's a good start.

00:23:36.830 --> 00:23:40.400
We also know that it's really not enough.

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:44.040
You need to also be getting
a pretty consistent dose

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:45.970
of vigorous exercise too.

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:46.850
It doesn't have to be a lot.

00:23:46.850 --> 00:23:49.640
A lot of these studies
show 10 minutes a day,

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:50.710
15 minutes a day,

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:53.250
even even 15 minutes a week
if you're getting started

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.540
is going to have health benefits.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:57.970
But vigorous exercise,

00:23:57.970 --> 00:23:59.240
the easiest way to think about it

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:01.797
is you your breathing rate goes up

00:24:01.797 --> 00:24:03.530
and your heart rate goes up, right?

00:24:03.530 --> 00:24:04.363
- Right.

00:24:04.363 --> 00:24:06.930
And there's a reason why vigorous exercise

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:10.550
is so important beyond
just walking and standing,

00:24:10.550 --> 00:24:12.420
it's that you have hypoxia.

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:16.590
Low levels of oxygen are
undoubtedly good for you,

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:18.340
even though they may not feel good.

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.490
And you know you're hypoxic

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.550
when you're panting so much

00:24:22.550 --> 00:24:24.830
you cannot carry out a conversation.

00:24:24.830 --> 00:24:25.920
That's what you're aiming for

00:24:25.920 --> 00:24:28.440
for at least 10 minutes
a few times a week.

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:30.940
So why is being out of breath important?

00:24:30.940 --> 00:24:32.110
Very rarely people ask that.

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:33.483
Well, the main reason-

00:24:33.483 --> 00:24:34.757
- 'Cause it doesn't seem
to make sense, honestly.

00:24:34.757 --> 00:24:36.920
Like, you need oxygen.
- Right.

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:38.690
So, again, it's this hormesis idea.

00:24:38.690 --> 00:24:41.490
A little bit of what doesn't
kill you makes you stronger.

00:24:41.490 --> 00:24:43.820
So what's happening is
when you're hypoxic,

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:47.320
is you're turning on a
what's called HIF-1alpha.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.090
hypoxia inducible factor,

00:24:50.090 --> 00:24:53.560
and that pathway turns on a
bunch of really helpful genes

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:56.890
that control new blood vessel
growth and mitochondria.

00:24:56.890 --> 00:25:00.080
My good friend and
colleague over at Harvard

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.720
won the Nobel prize in 2019

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:05.520
for discovering HIF-1alpha
and its role in biology.

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.450
Now, the other thing that happens in that;

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:08.283
there's two things.

00:25:08.283 --> 00:25:10.290
So one is they HIF-1alpha that
turns on a generic program,

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:11.260
that's good.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:14.350
But what also happens is you
get free radicals generated.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:16.460
Because when you don't have enough oxygen,

00:25:16.460 --> 00:25:19.810
those electrons that that
should normally be used

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:21.610
by what's called the
electron transport chain

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.620
in mitochondria, which generates ATP,

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:28.190
they fly out and they become
a super oxide radical.

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:29.750
- Can we talk about the electrons?

00:25:29.750 --> 00:25:31.565
'Cause it's really fascinating
how this thing works.

00:25:31.565 --> 00:25:35.000
I mean, it's like it is
a little circuit around-

00:25:35.000 --> 00:25:37.750
- Yeah, it's a little
battery made of proteins.

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:39.550
And there's a string of proteins

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:41.660
that come together in the membrane

00:25:41.660 --> 00:25:44.210
and they pass along an electron,

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:45.320
like a hot potato.

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:47.380
They don't want to hold onto
it, they shove it along:

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:48.680
Get rid of it, get rid of it.

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:50.620
And in doing so, they're pumping protons,

00:25:50.620 --> 00:25:52.460
which are hydrogen atoms,

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:54.480
into the inner membrane space.

00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:57.550
'Cause there's two membranes
that surround the mitochondria.

00:25:57.550 --> 00:25:59.540
And in that little space,

00:25:59.540 --> 00:26:01.380
the goal is to make it really acidic

00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.290
by putting in these protons into there,

00:26:03.290 --> 00:26:06.160
and this electron
transport chain does that.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:08.850
But you need oxygen because at the end,

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:09.810
when you get to the end,

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:11.060
you got to get rid of this electron

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:12.950
and they give it to oxygen, okay?

00:26:12.950 --> 00:26:14.253
And then that's called respirations.

00:26:14.253 --> 00:26:19.040
That's why we need to
breathe so hard when we run

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:20.890
and why we need oxygen in the first place.

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:22.670
Now, if you don't have enough oxygen,

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:25.290
then that'll lead to these
free radicals being produced

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:27.280
and it'll damage the cell.

00:26:27.280 --> 00:26:29.090
So you might say, "Well,
that makes no sense.

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:31.420
If you're running and
you're damaging the cell,

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:32.710
it's going to be bad, right?"

00:26:32.710 --> 00:26:35.230
But remember, a little
bit of damage can be good.

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:37.700
So what happens is it stimulates

00:26:37.700 --> 00:26:41.010
what's called mitohormesis,
mitochondrial hormesis.

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:44.320
And that has a whole
variety benefits as well,

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:47.220
including the manufacture
of more mitochondria

00:26:47.220 --> 00:26:49.060
that gives you energy.

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:50.150
But I want to mention something

00:26:50.150 --> 00:26:51.670
that's really interesting, I think,

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:54.340
about this electron transport chain.

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:55.910
Remember how I said those protons,

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:57.950
those hydrogens get pumped
into that little space

00:26:57.950 --> 00:26:59.540
to make it acidic?

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:01.230
So now you've got what's
called a gradient.

00:27:01.230 --> 00:27:03.240
You've got more of them in that space

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:06.350
than inside the mitochondria
in the inner bubble.

00:27:06.350 --> 00:27:08.290
They want to get out;

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:09.440
if they got too much somewhere,

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:10.990
they should equilibrate.

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:13.940
And what the cell does
is it puts little holes

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:16.870
that protons can escape
through, a little...

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:18.633
It's it's called the ATPase.

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:23.070
And what happens is as
those protons shoot through,

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:26.580
it's like a hydroelectric dam turbine.

00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:27.620
And as they shoot through,

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:29.530
it literally spins like a turbine.

00:27:29.530 --> 00:27:31.980
There's a little wheel at
the bottom of this tube

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:34.880
that spins super fast
many times per second,

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:39.270
and that spinning allows it
to make ATP or chemical energy

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:44.270
which then you use then to
burn to make the muscle work.

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:46.500
And so what's really interesting

00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.630
is that the more mitochondria you have,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:50.760
if you've been exercising for a few weeks,

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.470
allows you to make more chemical energy,

00:27:52.470 --> 00:27:55.830
and you don't feel puffed
as much as you did before.

00:27:55.830 --> 00:27:56.940
And the other thing that's happened

00:27:56.940 --> 00:27:58.530
besides having more mitochondria

00:27:58.530 --> 00:28:00.900
is that you've built more
blood vessels in your body,

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:03.620
and so your oxygen gets
transported to the tissues.

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:06.140
Now, both of those things
go awry as we get older.

00:28:06.140 --> 00:28:08.140
And we'll talk later and tomorrow

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:10.610
about how you can circumvent that

00:28:10.610 --> 00:28:13.890
and reverse your age to be
able to regain that ability.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.950
- With drugs, and
molecules, and supplements?

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:18.840
- Besides what we're talking
about today, exercise itself.

00:28:18.840 --> 00:28:19.673
- Yeah.

00:28:19.673 --> 00:28:20.890
We keep going back to this idea

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:23.720
of a little bit of
adversity is good for you.

00:28:23.720 --> 00:28:26.250
Like, a lot of anything's
going to kill you,

00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.180
but a little bit of a lot of these things

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:31.940
that might in big doses kill
you is really good for us

00:28:31.940 --> 00:28:34.020
'cause it puts our bodies
into the state of adversity.

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.230
Let's talk about this

00:28:35.230 --> 00:28:40.230
in terms of another effect of exercise,

00:28:40.434 --> 00:28:42.930
increased glucose sensitivity.

00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:43.763
- Right.

00:28:43.763 --> 00:28:44.630
So as you get older,

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.770
our muscles and our brain
become less sensitive

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:47.603
to the insulin.

00:28:47.603 --> 00:28:49.670
The pancreas is putting out
increasingly more and more

00:28:49.670 --> 00:28:52.870
trying to cope with this
insensitivity that happens.

00:28:52.870 --> 00:28:53.980
Now, that's largely,

00:28:53.980 --> 00:28:56.810
we think now because of
this aging biological clock,

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:58.490
that the genes that are required

00:28:58.490 --> 00:29:01.330
for muscle cell to function
or brain cell to function

00:29:01.330 --> 00:29:02.980
are getting switched on
and off in the wrong way.

00:29:02.980 --> 00:29:05.540
We call this ex-differentiation.

00:29:05.540 --> 00:29:07.350
And if you didn't see episode 1,

00:29:07.350 --> 00:29:10.370
you might want to go back
and hear more about that.

00:29:10.370 --> 00:29:13.320
Basically cells are losing
their identity as they get older

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:16.460
and not putting out a
particular protein called GLUT4,

00:29:16.460 --> 00:29:19.510
which is a glucose transporter,
on the outside of the cells.

00:29:19.510 --> 00:29:21.980
So the muscles are not
bringing in the glucose.

00:29:21.980 --> 00:29:22.900
That's two problems, right?

00:29:22.900 --> 00:29:23.733
First of all,

00:29:23.733 --> 00:29:25.890
you don't have your energy
to burn in the mitochondria

00:29:25.890 --> 00:29:27.000
to make energy.

00:29:27.000 --> 00:29:28.760
The other problem is that that sugar,

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:30.610
that glucose that circulates
in your bloodstream

00:29:30.610 --> 00:29:33.420
and doesn't get taken up into
your muscle and your brain

00:29:33.420 --> 00:29:35.490
starts to damage the lining
of the blood vessels,

00:29:35.490 --> 00:29:38.010
and we know that is type 2 diabetes

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:39.490
and cardiovascular disease.

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:42.350
- And that's where VEGF comes in?

00:29:42.350 --> 00:29:45.010
- So VEGF is a little protein

00:29:45.010 --> 00:29:48.540
that signals the formation
of new blood vessels.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:49.430
When you exercise-

00:29:49.430 --> 00:29:51.081
- How do you spell this VEGF, by the way?

00:29:51.081 --> 00:29:52.750
- V-E-G-F.

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:54.052
- Okay, so if people want to look this up.

00:29:54.052 --> 00:29:55.250
Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:29:55.250 --> 00:29:56.083
- Okay. All right.

00:29:56.083 --> 00:29:56.916
So VEGF?

00:29:56.916 --> 00:29:59.500
- So VEGF is made by
muscles after you exercise;

00:29:59.500 --> 00:30:01.560
if you've gone for your
run or even your walk,

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:03.460
but running is even better.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:06.320
And there's a gene called PGC-1alpha,

00:30:06.320 --> 00:30:08.290
that is what's called
a transcription factor.

00:30:08.290 --> 00:30:09.420
It'll be made,

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:13.710
it'll bind to the upstream
side of the VEGF gene

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:16.310
and make more of this VEGF protein.

00:30:16.310 --> 00:30:20.430
Now, VEGF will be secreted
out of the muscle and diffuse

00:30:20.430 --> 00:30:24.700
or just leak out into the
lining of blood vessels.

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:27.070
Now, the lining of the blood
vessels is made of cells

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:29.980
called endothelial cells
which pick up the signal,

00:30:29.980 --> 00:30:32.670
they have little
receptors that sense VEGF.

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:34.757
And when they get that signal that says,

00:30:34.757 --> 00:30:39.240
"Oh wow, we're being chased
by a saber tooth tiger

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:40.073
for the last week,

00:30:40.073 --> 00:30:41.790
we better build more blood vessels,"

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:42.840
and they do that.

00:30:42.840 --> 00:30:45.170
Normally, if you have a
tube which is a capillary,

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:48.560
almost microscopic blood vessels,

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:49.960
what happens is the endothelial cells

00:30:49.960 --> 00:30:51.510
will start to branch out to the side

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:53.120
and make a new blood vessel.

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:55.440
And that's what you get with exercise.

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:57.640
Conversely, if you sit around all day,

00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:59.070
you won't build new blood vessels,

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:01.280
you'll actually have fewer and fewer

00:31:01.280 --> 00:31:02.570
and your muscle and your brain

00:31:02.570 --> 00:31:04.610
starts to get starved of oxygen.

00:31:04.610 --> 00:31:08.070
Now, one of the big problems
we discovered is during aging,

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:09.260
that signaling pathway,

00:31:09.260 --> 00:31:12.020
that signaling mechanism is defective.

00:31:12.020 --> 00:31:14.460
And even though you're
exercising as you get older,

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:15.960
you're not getting the benefits of it;

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:17.580
you don't get the new blood vessels.

00:31:17.580 --> 00:31:18.920
- And you can see this in a mouse.

00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:22.370
I mean, when you pull the mouse apart,

00:31:22.370 --> 00:31:23.380
not to be too graphic,

00:31:23.380 --> 00:31:24.580
but you can actually see

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:27.010
these thinning vessels.
- We don't even need

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:28.480
to hurt the mouse to see this.

00:31:28.480 --> 00:31:31.370
We can use imaging to see
the blood flow in a mouse

00:31:31.370 --> 00:31:32.400
without hurting it,

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:33.960
and we can see the bright red areas

00:31:33.960 --> 00:31:35.670
where the blood's flowing nicely.

00:31:35.670 --> 00:31:39.260
And in an old mouse, you don't
get that even with exercise.

00:31:39.260 --> 00:31:43.610
But we found a way to restore
the youth of the muscle

00:31:43.610 --> 00:31:45.670
and restore the ability of that VEGF

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:47.890
to trigger new blood vessel formation.

00:31:47.890 --> 00:31:52.490
And the trick was to turn
on the production of NAD,

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.200
which is the fuel for the
sirtuin survival circuit.

00:31:55.200 --> 00:31:58.190
- Which can be turned on by exercise

00:31:58.190 --> 00:32:01.800
because exercise upregulates NAMPT.

00:32:01.800 --> 00:32:03.590
- Yeah, also known as NAMPT.

00:32:03.590 --> 00:32:05.350
- NAMPT.
- Some people call it that.

00:32:05.350 --> 00:32:06.183
But, yeah,

00:32:06.183 --> 00:32:08.860
this is the relative of
that ancient yeast gene

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:11.240
that we talked about in a first episode

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:13.570
that extends lifespan of yeast cells.

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:16.020
- So let's add one more piece to this,

00:32:16.020 --> 00:32:21.020
and that is how exercise
impacts the epigenome.

00:32:21.340 --> 00:32:22.260
- Yeah, the epigenome...

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:23.540
If you missed episode 1,

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:26.170
I encourage you to go listen
to that and watch that

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:28.390
because we covered what the epigenome is

00:32:28.390 --> 00:32:30.970
and how it affects the aging process.

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:33.410
But the summary is that the epigenome

00:32:33.410 --> 00:32:35.200
is the regulator of the DNA.

00:32:35.200 --> 00:32:37.107
It tells the genes whether
it'd be on and off.

00:32:37.107 --> 00:32:39.000
And that goes wrong during aging,

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.050
and may actually be the reason we age.

00:32:41.050 --> 00:32:43.030
And one of the reasons we believe that

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.560
is that you can measure
biological age now in people.

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:46.900
I could take one of your cells, Matt,

00:32:46.900 --> 00:32:48.010
back to my lab.

00:32:48.010 --> 00:32:50.010
Well, actually I have to
take a chunk of your cells

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.350
or cheek swab,

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:54.480
and we can measure the rate of your aging.

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:57.800
It's called the Horvath clock
or DNA methylation clock.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:32:59.080
And when you look at people

00:32:59.080 --> 00:33:01.430
that have lived a healthy lifestyle,

00:33:01.430 --> 00:33:04.110
say they've been exercising
for the last 30 years.

00:33:04.110 --> 00:33:06.120
they are much younger epigenetically,

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:07.030
according to this clock,

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:08.500
than somebody who hasn't.

00:33:08.500 --> 00:33:11.480
So that tells us that
very likely that exercise

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:14.320
is not just protecting you
against cardiovascular disease,

00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:16.480
it's slowing down your
overall rate of aging.

00:33:16.480 --> 00:33:18.799
- There are other clocks as well.

00:33:18.799 --> 00:33:20.336
One of our-
- There are many actually.

00:33:20.336 --> 00:33:22.770
- So one of our researchers, Dave Johnson,

00:33:22.770 --> 00:33:25.160
was involved in a study that
used the proteomic clock,

00:33:25.160 --> 00:33:26.760
which works similarly.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:27.820
Let's talk about that really quick.

00:33:27.820 --> 00:33:31.190
'Cause it's another way to
measure biological aging.

00:33:31.190 --> 00:33:32.023
- Right.

00:33:32.023 --> 00:33:33.490
So you can measure a
proteins in the blood,

00:33:33.490 --> 00:33:35.980
which change predictably over time.

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:39.210
There's a protein called
GDF15, which is circulating...

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:42.040
It's now a really great
biomarker for aging.

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:43.010
In fact,

00:33:43.010 --> 00:33:44.810
GDF15 is one of those genes

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:48.160
that was used in that study
we referred to last episode,

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:51.290
where the Mediterranean diet
slowed the aging process.

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:53.050
They found that GDF15,

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:56.890
the methylation on that clock
marker was also slowed down.

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.970
The point here being that
these proteomic clocks

00:33:59.970 --> 00:34:01.200
and the epigenetic clocks,

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:03.240
and some others like an immuno clock,

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:06.870
can be used now to understand
what slows down aging

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:07.930
but, importantly,

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:10.560
how do you reverse it
with various treatments?

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:13.160
And we've gone from a
field where we had no idea

00:34:13.160 --> 00:34:14.810
what was going on in aging;

00:34:14.810 --> 00:34:17.990
really, we had to look at
tens of thousands of people

00:34:17.990 --> 00:34:20.820
on metformin to see if it helped health

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:22.540
to a point where we can
take a hundred people,

00:34:22.540 --> 00:34:24.200
treat them with something;

00:34:24.200 --> 00:34:26.570
two months later, see if
they've gotten younger or not.

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:28.820
And those experiments
are being done right now.

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:30.690
- And one of the things
we could treat them with

00:34:30.690 --> 00:34:31.530
is exercise.

00:34:31.530 --> 00:34:35.120
Exercise, in the study we
were just talking about,

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:38.700
they looked at aerobically active people

00:34:38.700 --> 00:34:40.150
versus sedentary people,

00:34:40.150 --> 00:34:41.690
and the aerobically active people

00:34:41.690 --> 00:34:44.290
were five and a half
years younger on average,

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:47.000
according to this
proteomic biological clock.

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:48.430
Presumably we'd see the same thing

00:34:48.430 --> 00:34:51.400
if we used a Horvath
clock or an immuno clock.

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:52.760
- Yeah. And that's true, actually.

00:34:52.760 --> 00:34:54.710
It's been done on exercised people

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.280
and they are also epigenetically young.

00:34:57.280 --> 00:34:59.040
And what's important about
this clock, to realize,

00:34:59.040 --> 00:35:01.380
is that it doesn't just tell
you your biological age.

00:35:01.380 --> 00:35:03.060
You can actually predict
when you're going to die

00:35:03.060 --> 00:35:04.580
or when someone's going to die.

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:07.210
And when I say that some people

00:35:07.210 --> 00:35:08.667
probably got uncomfortable.
- It's a little morbid.

00:35:08.667 --> 00:35:09.898
- "I don't want to know this."

00:35:09.898 --> 00:35:10.960
Well, consider this,

00:35:10.960 --> 00:35:14.181
that 80% of your future
health is in your own hands,

00:35:14.181 --> 00:35:16.240
it's modifiable;

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:19.550
20% is genetic, which you
can't do much about yet.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:20.693
That's powerful.

00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:25.140
If you have the tools to
change your rate of aging...

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:25.973
First of all,

00:35:25.973 --> 00:35:27.320
you have to measure it
to know if it's working.

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:28.310
So I recommend that,

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:31.180
and we'll talk about ways
to do that practically.

00:35:31.180 --> 00:35:35.900
But once you have that
number, then work with me.

00:35:35.900 --> 00:35:37.560
We can tell you here in podcast

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:40.590
how to actually slow and
reverse that process.

00:35:40.590 --> 00:35:42.993
- Well, we use car
analogies a lot, you and I.

00:35:42.993 --> 00:35:44.530
I think a lot of people do these.

00:35:44.530 --> 00:35:47.180
But it bears mentioning, like,

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.773
you know that your car's
not going to last forever.

00:35:51.210 --> 00:35:53.080
There's a shelf life for any car.

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:56.370
You get to choose based on how
you treat the car over time

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:57.203
whether or not the car

00:35:57.203 --> 00:35:59.960
is going to last 100,000
miles or 200,000 miles

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:00.937
or 3 or 400,000 miles.

00:36:00.937 --> 00:36:03.157
- Right, take it to the body shop repair

00:36:03.157 --> 00:36:04.940
and the mechanics more often.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:06.895
That's what we're talking
about for our bodies.

00:36:06.895 --> 00:36:07.728
Don't just let things fall apart.

00:36:07.728 --> 00:36:08.990
- And that's why knowing is important.

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:10.220
- Well, you need to measure it?

00:36:10.220 --> 00:36:12.630
You need to know when
the car needs a service.

00:36:12.630 --> 00:36:15.880
You need to know, if
you're tweaking that knob,

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:18.110
whether you're doing harm or good.

00:36:18.110 --> 00:36:19.790
- And to that end,

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:22.940
one of the really cool
and exciting developments

00:36:22.940 --> 00:36:23.950
that's happening right now

00:36:23.950 --> 00:36:28.660
is there's this sort of
race for these clocks

00:36:28.660 --> 00:36:32.083
to become available
widely for people to use,

00:36:34.239 --> 00:36:36.170
so much so that the price point

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:40.260
is predicted to be, what, like buck?

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:41.740
- Well, it's coming down the whole time.

00:36:41.740 --> 00:36:43.170
- For a Horvath test?
- Right.

00:36:43.170 --> 00:36:45.670
So right now if you want to
check your biological age,

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:49.130
you can go to InsideTracker,
one of our kind sponsors,

00:36:49.130 --> 00:36:50.860
that's a blood test;

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:52.370
or you can also have a DNA test,

00:36:52.370 --> 00:36:55.150
which is available by
for a few hundred dollars

00:36:55.150 --> 00:36:57.030
from various vendors.

00:36:57.030 --> 00:36:58.920
A student in my lab, Patrick Griffin,

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.400
we've posted recently a paper,

00:37:01.400 --> 00:37:05.780
which can be accessed on bioarchive.org,

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:09.160
that describes a way
to pool, put together,

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.790
thousands of people's samples
and run them all together,

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:14.910
bringing the cost of that test
down to less than a dollar,

00:37:14.910 --> 00:37:17.210
which means that very
shortly in the future

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:18.410
when we bring out this product,

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:20.630
and we are bringing it out soon,

00:37:20.630 --> 00:37:23.750
you should be able to
do this test routinely

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:27.070
and see whether you try
new diet or a supplement

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:28.030
or just meditation,

00:37:28.030 --> 00:37:30.280
whether you're actually
benefiting from that.

00:37:31.690 --> 00:37:33.977
- A lot of people hear
all of this and they go,

00:37:33.977 --> 00:37:36.987
"Okay, I get it, I have to exercise, but,"

00:37:36.987 --> 00:37:38.017
and I hear this a lot,

00:37:38.017 --> 00:37:39.370
"but I hate to run."

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:41.200
Like, everybody thinks
that that's the only thing

00:37:41.200 --> 00:37:42.070
they're allowed to do.

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:45.820
And I think physicians may
contribute to this a little bit

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:46.653
by saying, like,

00:37:46.653 --> 00:37:48.320
"Okay, well, I need to get
you running and jogging,"

00:37:48.320 --> 00:37:49.153
and whatever.

00:37:50.490 --> 00:37:51.830
It's not just running, right?

00:37:51.830 --> 00:37:54.270
Like, what's the best kind of exercise?

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:56.480
- Well, there isn't one-

00:37:56.480 --> 00:37:57.313
- It's the exercise you're going to do.

00:37:57.313 --> 00:37:59.120
- It's what you love doing.

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:00.774
And it can be a sport.

00:38:00.774 --> 00:38:05.600
It could be wind surfing,
it could be sailing;

00:38:05.600 --> 00:38:07.310
but just move, that's the main thing.

00:38:07.310 --> 00:38:09.430
There's even sex, which is...

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:11.773
We actually looked this up.

00:38:11.773 --> 00:38:12.606
It's not as good as we thought.

00:38:12.606 --> 00:38:13.439
- We did look this up.

00:38:13.439 --> 00:38:14.790
We were really hopeful
when we looked this up,

00:38:14.790 --> 00:38:17.140
but it burns like four
calories a minute or something.

00:38:17.140 --> 00:38:18.950
It's not as good as-

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:20.860
- Well, I'm sure it depends
on the individual, right?

00:38:20.860 --> 00:38:24.410
- I mean, you can make sex more aerobic.

00:38:24.410 --> 00:38:26.390
You can make it part of
your exercise regimen,

00:38:26.390 --> 00:38:27.920
but you have to have a partner;

00:38:27.920 --> 00:38:29.270
a consenting, willing partner;

00:38:29.270 --> 00:38:32.540
who also wants to make it part
of their exercise regimen.

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:35.260
- It's only 4.2 calories
per minute, which...

00:38:35.260 --> 00:38:39.630
And we also looked up the
average length of sex,

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:41.312
and it's about five minutes.

00:38:41.312 --> 00:38:42.145
So that's not a lot.

00:38:42.145 --> 00:38:43.580
- So you wold have burned 20 calories

00:38:43.580 --> 00:38:45.670
if you're an average person.

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:46.760
I'll burn 10.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:47.980
- So Instead of that,

00:38:47.980 --> 00:38:51.030
you probably need to go for
a longer walk afterwards

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:52.760
to really burn off those calories

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:53.780
and get your blood flowing.

00:38:53.780 --> 00:38:55.950
- And however you get your exercise,

00:38:55.950 --> 00:39:00.143
be it running or sailing,
apparently, or sex,

00:39:01.310 --> 00:39:02.760
both the World Health Organization

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:06.290
and the Mayo clinic
recommend at least 75 minutes

00:39:06.290 --> 00:39:08.050
of vigorous exercise a week,

00:39:08.050 --> 00:39:11.120
so that's 15 minutes a day, 10 minutes-

00:39:11.120 --> 00:39:13.150
- And it's better if you
spread it out over the week.

00:39:13.150 --> 00:39:15.920
- Right, yeah, don't do it all at once.

00:39:15.920 --> 00:39:17.700
But that's the starting point.

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:19.180
And most people can do that.

00:39:19.180 --> 00:39:20.920
You can be the busiest
person in the world,

00:39:20.920 --> 00:39:23.420
you can be the most unhealthy
person in the world,

00:39:24.320 --> 00:39:27.360
you can get your heart
rate and your breathing up

00:39:27.360 --> 00:39:28.700
for 10 minutes a day.

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:29.533
- Sure, you can.

00:39:29.533 --> 00:39:30.400
And the benefits will be huge.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:31.360
You'll feel better,

00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:33.000
you'll look better,

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:35.180
and you will also have a
better resting heart rate.

00:39:35.180 --> 00:39:38.050
That's one of the best
measures of your fitness.

00:39:38.050 --> 00:39:40.700
If you're less than 50
for a resting heart rate,

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:41.690
that's really good.

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:43.124
For someone my age, in my 50s,

00:39:43.124 --> 00:39:46.310
mine's usually around mid-40s.

00:39:46.310 --> 00:39:48.960
That's a good indication
of how well you're doing.

00:39:48.960 --> 00:39:51.390
And if you've got a high heart rate,

00:39:51.390 --> 00:39:53.170
it's you're either too
stressed or you're unfit,

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:54.690
try to bring that down again.

00:39:54.690 --> 00:39:56.040
You can measure your
heart rate pretty easily,

00:39:56.040 --> 00:39:57.540
just put your hand on your arm.

00:39:57.540 --> 00:40:00.430
But you could also either wear
a ring or a new fitness...

00:40:00.430 --> 00:40:01.592
these fitness apps-

00:40:01.592 --> 00:40:03.200
- There's all kind of wearables now

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:04.340
that are going to help you do this.

00:40:04.340 --> 00:40:06.920
- Yeah, and I'm a big proponent
of measuring things, right?

00:40:06.920 --> 00:40:08.180
'Cause otherwise you're flying blind.

00:40:08.180 --> 00:40:12.370
So I wear a ring, I wear a wrist watch.

00:40:12.370 --> 00:40:13.330
I sometimes wear-

00:40:13.330 --> 00:40:14.354
- You're wearing right now.

00:40:14.354 --> 00:40:15.450
- I am.

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.020
This also measures sleep,

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:19.453
which we'll get to in another episode.

00:40:19.453 --> 00:40:21.180
There are some advanced bio monitors

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:22.340
we're also going to cover.

00:40:22.340 --> 00:40:24.440
There's one that I stick
on my chest for two weeks

00:40:24.440 --> 00:40:26.320
that measures my body a
thousand times a second

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:28.760
for various parameters,
including my heart function.

00:40:28.760 --> 00:40:29.593
And in the future,

00:40:29.593 --> 00:40:32.250
these will be used by people
to predict heart attacks

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:33.590
as well as the flu,

00:40:33.590 --> 00:40:36.360
and all sorts of issues like depression.

00:40:36.360 --> 00:40:37.980
In the future, we're not
going to be flying blind,

00:40:37.980 --> 00:40:40.230
we're going to be monitored
a thousand times a second,

00:40:40.230 --> 00:40:42.710
as opposed to now where you
go to the doctor once a year

00:40:42.710 --> 00:40:44.910
and they spend 15 minutes
asking you, "How do you feel?"

00:40:44.910 --> 00:40:45.890
which is a joke.

00:40:45.890 --> 00:40:46.913
- Right, right.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.174
Because in addition to the fact

00:40:49.174 --> 00:40:52.550
that it's a single
assessment at a single time,

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:53.710
it's highly subjective,

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:55.080
it's self-reported.

00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:56.920
This is just crap data.
- Yeah.

00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:57.753
This has a bit of an side,

00:40:57.753 --> 00:40:59.040
but it's worth noting here

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:01.960
that I've been measuring
myself for over 10 years

00:41:01.960 --> 00:41:03.080
using InsideTracker.

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.840
And that data is wonderful.

00:41:04.840 --> 00:41:07.370
It's graphed and you can
see when things go wrong,

00:41:07.370 --> 00:41:08.383
I've corrected it.

00:41:09.340 --> 00:41:11.650
They do an inner age study.

00:41:11.650 --> 00:41:13.380
There's a measure called Inner Age 2.0

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:15.080
which tells you how you're doing

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:16.490
compared to others your age,

00:41:16.490 --> 00:41:19.100
and you try to keep that low.

00:41:19.100 --> 00:41:20.590
And based on that calculation,

00:41:20.590 --> 00:41:23.190
I've been getting younger
for the last 10 years,

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:24.680
which is great to talk about

00:41:24.680 --> 00:41:28.560
but it also means that I'm
optimizing my body every time

00:41:28.560 --> 00:41:29.480
when I do something;

00:41:29.480 --> 00:41:32.010
and if something doesn't
work, then I won't do.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:33.150
- And we are going to get into this,

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:33.983
but I do want to...

00:41:33.983 --> 00:41:35.100
I've got a friend, Nate Price,

00:41:35.100 --> 00:41:36.340
who helps people do this

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:38.230
at the Institute for Systems Biology,

00:41:38.230 --> 00:41:41.620
and they help people create these charts

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:44.180
so that they could then bring
in to show their doctors.

00:41:44.180 --> 00:41:45.790
And he was telling me about this woman

00:41:45.790 --> 00:41:47.240
who brought her chart into the doctor.

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:49.227
And the doctor was at first
dismissive and she said,

00:41:49.227 --> 00:41:51.060
"Well, won't you at least look at it?"

00:41:51.060 --> 00:41:52.887
And the doctor looked at it and went,

00:41:52.887 --> 00:41:54.850
"Oh wow, this is really helpful."

00:41:54.850 --> 00:41:57.280
- That's exactly the
response of my doctor too.

00:41:57.280 --> 00:42:00.777
My doctor said, "Well, how do you feel?"

00:42:00.777 --> 00:42:02.450
and I said, "I'm good, I'm sleeping well."

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:03.283
And he said, "Okay, see you next time."

00:42:03.283 --> 00:42:04.690
I said, "No, hang on.

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:05.523
Let's do some more.

00:42:05.523 --> 00:42:07.130
I've got some data, can I show you?"

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.840
And I showed him the
InsideTracker data on Zoom

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:10.930
and he said, "This is wonderful.

00:42:10.930 --> 00:42:12.970
I wish all my patients had this data."

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:17.080
Of course, he cannot order
43 biomarkers every time.

00:42:17.080 --> 00:42:19.460
The health insurance won't pay for it.

00:42:19.460 --> 00:42:20.350
But individuals,

00:42:20.350 --> 00:42:21.610
if they want to spend their money;

00:42:21.610 --> 00:42:24.570
instead of coffee, they can
put it into a blood test.

00:42:24.570 --> 00:42:26.890
Doctors actually do respect those data

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:29.410
because they're actually
produced by the same companies

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:31.160
that the doctors would use anyway.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:32.510
- And to bring this all back...

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:34.140
To tie this all back to exercise

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:35.610
and these other adversity mimetics,

00:42:35.610 --> 00:42:38.500
which we're going to get
to, having this data...

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:39.700
I'm a big soccer fan.

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:42.540
The first time I saw a soccer
player take off his shirt,

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:43.587
he was wearing a bra and I thought,

00:42:43.587 --> 00:42:45.070
"What the heck is going on?"

00:42:45.070 --> 00:42:48.110
but it's actually holding these trackers

00:42:48.110 --> 00:42:50.300
that are measuring things
in the middle of games,

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:51.710
in the middle of practices.

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:53.910
The world's best athletes
are doing this right now

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:55.730
and it's becoming more and more available

00:42:55.730 --> 00:42:57.290
for other people to track as well.

00:42:57.290 --> 00:42:59.000
And once you have this data,

00:42:59.000 --> 00:43:00.860
you can start to act on this data.

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:05.030
You can start to arrange
your exercise routine

00:43:05.030 --> 00:43:07.030
so that you're getting the most out of it.

00:43:07.030 --> 00:43:08.630
- Exactly. And they can be fun too.

00:43:08.630 --> 00:43:10.740
You can use GPS to see where you're going.

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:13.517
You can compare it with your
colleagues, your friends:

00:43:13.517 --> 00:43:15.130
"I'm running more than you."

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:18.750
- Let's move along here

00:43:18.750 --> 00:43:20.670
because we've got a
lot more to talk about.

00:43:20.670 --> 00:43:22.093
But before we get away from exercise

00:43:22.093 --> 00:43:24.843
and start talking about these
other adversity mimetics,

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:28.190
there's another form of
exercise that I know you enjoy,

00:43:28.190 --> 00:43:29.870
that I enjoy, that a lot of people enjoy,

00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:31.940
but maybe doesn't get as much attention

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:33.120
as aerobic exercises,

00:43:33.120 --> 00:43:34.430
is weight training.

00:43:34.430 --> 00:43:35.660
- I'm glad you said that.

00:43:35.660 --> 00:43:36.590
All right, so weight training.

00:43:36.590 --> 00:43:39.180
Yeah, I do that fairly often;

00:43:39.180 --> 00:43:43.300
I would say at least every
other day, if not every day.

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:45.732
The way I do that is I
have a gym in my bedroom

00:43:45.732 --> 00:43:47.560
and I also have weights next to my desk.

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:49.360
- You also have them in the
trunk of your car right now

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:50.890
I saw.
- I do.

00:43:50.890 --> 00:43:52.597
It's 'cause I travel a lot,

00:43:52.597 --> 00:43:54.660
and so I'm taking weights with me.

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:56.480
But the reason that I do weightlifting

00:43:56.480 --> 00:43:59.300
is that it's going to
maintain my hormone levels,

00:43:59.300 --> 00:44:01.970
it's going to maintain
my ability to walk well

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:04.300
and stay upright and have good posture.

00:44:04.300 --> 00:44:06.022
It makes me look better,

00:44:06.022 --> 00:44:08.760
although you may say that's debatable.

00:44:08.760 --> 00:44:10.465
But also, as I get older-

00:44:10.465 --> 00:44:11.298
- It's not debatable.

00:44:11.298 --> 00:44:12.420
You're really good looking guy, David.

00:44:12.420 --> 00:44:13.253
- Thanks.

00:44:13.253 --> 00:44:14.086
You're lovely, Matthew.

00:44:14.086 --> 00:44:14.919
- [Matthew] Thank you.

00:44:14.919 --> 00:44:17.170
- The other thing that
is often not appreciated

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:20.390
is it's harder to build
muscle as you get older,

00:44:20.390 --> 00:44:22.360
and you want to maintain that muscle mass.

00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:24.770
I'm losing about 1% of
my muscle mass every year

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:27.550
unless I maintain it or build it up.

00:44:27.550 --> 00:44:30.270
As you get older, you're
going to fall over, right?

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:32.980
Everyone falls over,
especially as they get older.

00:44:32.980 --> 00:44:36.360
And if you break your hip,
that's close to a death sentence.

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:38.060
A lot of people...

00:44:38.060 --> 00:44:41.370
Well, 19 people every minute fall over

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:43.370
and break their hip in the United States,

00:44:43.370 --> 00:44:45.920
which is a major cause of mortality in-

00:44:45.920 --> 00:44:47.860
- Yeah, the mortality
rates after a hip break

00:44:47.860 --> 00:44:49.170
just like skyrocket.

00:44:49.170 --> 00:44:52.260
- Well, it's equivalent to
having metastatic cancer.

00:44:52.260 --> 00:44:53.550
That's how bad it is.

00:44:53.550 --> 00:44:55.740
But if you've got muscles
and you're flexible,

00:44:55.740 --> 00:44:59.480
you do, say, Pilates in
your older age and stretch,

00:44:59.480 --> 00:45:02.550
build up the muscles
particularly around your waist,

00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:05.550
then you're much less likely
to break a bone when you fall.

00:45:05.550 --> 00:45:06.550
- Building muscle mass

00:45:06.550 --> 00:45:09.740
also helps maintain
youthful hormone level?

00:45:09.740 --> 00:45:10.573
- Right.

00:45:10.573 --> 00:45:12.230
So I had trouble maintaining
testosterone levels

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:14.080
like most men my age.

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:16.930
Now, there are two ways
to go about it generally.

00:45:16.930 --> 00:45:19.740
The main one is that your doctor
will give you an injection

00:45:19.740 --> 00:45:23.340
of testosterone to apply at home

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:24.990
or a cream that you can
rub on your forearm.

00:45:24.990 --> 00:45:27.780
Those are the two ways you can do it.

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:28.790
There are others.

00:45:28.790 --> 00:45:31.510
But the other way, which I prefer,

00:45:31.510 --> 00:45:35.203
is to naturally boost it by
maintaining large muscles,

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:40.390
exercising my thighs, my butt, my back.

00:45:40.390 --> 00:45:41.340
And I've compared the two.

00:45:41.340 --> 00:45:43.660
I have tried cream on
my arm for testosterone,

00:45:43.660 --> 00:45:47.640
it worked okay, my levels
went up maybe by 20%.

00:45:47.640 --> 00:45:49.530
It wasn't great.

00:45:49.530 --> 00:45:52.070
I really maxed out my testosterone levels

00:45:52.070 --> 00:45:55.050
by doing leg exercise and back exercises.

00:45:55.050 --> 00:45:58.530
I'm talking about things like
leg extensions, leg curls,

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:00.060
as well as hip hinge,

00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:02.200
which has bending at the
hip and lifting weights.

00:46:02.200 --> 00:46:03.730
And I also do deadlifts,

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:05.030
which is standing up,

00:46:05.030 --> 00:46:06.980
lifting a really heavy weight

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:08.150
with a straight back.

00:46:08.150 --> 00:46:09.613
- There's another way that we know

00:46:09.613 --> 00:46:13.130
that exercise is impacting aging.

00:46:13.130 --> 00:46:17.070
And we talked about senescent cells

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:21.700
in terms of what iron may be doing

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:24.840
to cause cells to go senescent.

00:46:24.840 --> 00:46:28.730
But let's talk about that
as one of the key aging...

00:46:28.730 --> 00:46:31.430
what is called the aging hallmarks.

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:34.860
Exercise prevents senescence,
it turns back senescence?

00:46:34.860 --> 00:46:38.400
What's exercise doing in
terms of these zombie cells?

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:40.770
- Well, let's start with
what a senescent cell is.

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:42.837
So when the epigenome
becomes too dysregulated

00:46:42.837 --> 00:46:45.790
and this ex-differentiation
process happens,

00:46:45.790 --> 00:46:48.610
cells can either die,
they can become a cancer/

00:46:48.610 --> 00:46:51.520
But what the body tries
to do is to prevent that

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:53.210
by shutting these cells down

00:46:53.210 --> 00:46:54.660
and make them more like zombies;

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:56.650
they're alive but they're not dividing,

00:46:56.650 --> 00:46:57.820
and they're causing havoc.

00:46:57.820 --> 00:47:00.140
- They're excreting all these chemicals

00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:01.280
into the bloodstream.
- They are.

00:47:01.280 --> 00:47:02.390
It's called the SASP,

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:05.380
the senescence associated
secretory phenotype.

00:47:05.380 --> 00:47:08.440
And these proteins cause inflammation,

00:47:08.440 --> 00:47:09.930
they also cause cancer.

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:12.000
So the fewer senescent
cells you have in your body,

00:47:12.000 --> 00:47:13.100
the better.

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:15.600
And we're starting to find that lifestyle

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:17.950
and supplementation and some drugs

00:47:17.950 --> 00:47:20.660
can slow their formation;

00:47:20.660 --> 00:47:22.850
remember, keep the iron levels low;

00:47:22.850 --> 00:47:25.260
but also we can kill them off.

00:47:25.260 --> 00:47:27.700
And there's this study, the
one you're referring to,

00:47:27.700 --> 00:47:31.940
which took 34 individuals for
a 12 week exercise program

00:47:31.940 --> 00:47:33.710
and looked at the amount
of senescent cells

00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:37.140
circulating in the blood immune system,

00:47:37.140 --> 00:47:40.240
and it was dramatically lower
in the exercised people,

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:42.680
arguing that you can actually kill off

00:47:42.680 --> 00:47:44.340
or reverse that process.

00:47:44.340 --> 00:47:45.200
- [Matthew] 12 weeks.

00:47:45.200 --> 00:47:46.120
- Just 12 weeks.

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:48.800
- 'Cause these 12 weeks to
destroy some senescent cells

00:47:48.800 --> 00:47:50.970
seems like a pretty good deal.

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:51.910
- Yeah, it's one of the best ways

00:47:51.910 --> 00:47:53.050
to kill off senescent cells.

00:47:53.050 --> 00:47:55.590
- That's the study that
was just out this year

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:58.910
in "Aging Cell," Englund et al.

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:01.020
We will link to that in the show notes.

00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:04.000
Let's put a wrap on exercise.

00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:06.970
But let's have a few takeaways here.

00:48:06.970 --> 00:48:09.273
And there's really,
there's three big ones.

00:48:10.218 --> 00:48:13.285
The first is a low level of exercise,

00:48:13.285 --> 00:48:16.430
and this is just steps, right?

00:48:16.430 --> 00:48:17.263
- Get off your butt.

00:48:17.263 --> 00:48:18.130
- [Matthew] Get off your butt and take-

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:18.963
Yeah.

00:48:18.963 --> 00:48:21.480
- Standing desk or a
desk with a treadmill.

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:24.410
Walk around the block
after dinner or lunch.

00:48:24.410 --> 00:48:25.558
Those are the simple things.

00:48:25.558 --> 00:48:27.150
- And you don't have to hit 10,000 steps,

00:48:27.150 --> 00:48:28.119
but shoot for-

00:48:28.119 --> 00:48:29.480
- Four.
- Four.

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:30.480
Four's great.
- [David] Yeah.

00:48:30.480 --> 00:48:31.630
- Okay. All right.

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:35.040
And then that's the low intensity exercise

00:48:35.040 --> 00:48:36.560
that everybody should be
getting throughout the week,

00:48:36.560 --> 00:48:37.393
every single day.

00:48:37.393 --> 00:48:39.420
You should also get
high intensity exercise.

00:48:39.420 --> 00:48:40.253
- For sure.

00:48:40.253 --> 00:48:43.010
A few times a week, 10 or 15
minutes, lose your breath.

00:48:43.010 --> 00:48:44.250
It can be in the form of HIIT,

00:48:44.250 --> 00:48:45.980
high intensity interval training,

00:48:45.980 --> 00:48:47.550
or running on a treadmill.

00:48:47.550 --> 00:48:49.700
That will get your blood vessels flowing

00:48:49.700 --> 00:48:51.180
and your mitochondria amplifying.

00:48:51.180 --> 00:48:53.570
- 10 to 15 minutes a day.

00:48:53.570 --> 00:48:55.730
Yeah, that's enough.
- 75 minutes a week.

00:48:55.730 --> 00:48:57.900
- Yeah, that's what all
the experts recommend.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:48:58.733
- Okay. All right.

00:48:58.733 --> 00:49:01.400
And then muscle building.

00:49:01.400 --> 00:49:03.610
- Yeah, weights, that's just as important.

00:49:03.610 --> 00:49:06.270
So maintain that muscle
mass for your hormones,

00:49:06.270 --> 00:49:08.900
testosterone particularly
for men and women.

00:49:08.900 --> 00:49:10.440
Exercise the big muscles particularly,

00:49:10.440 --> 00:49:11.540
don't forget about those.

00:49:11.540 --> 00:49:14.010
Don't just work on your
upper arm so you look good.

00:49:14.010 --> 00:49:14.843
But it's pretty simple.

00:49:14.843 --> 00:49:16.720
You don't need a gym in
your bedroom like I do,

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:19.238
you can do pushups, sit ups,

00:49:19.238 --> 00:49:20.940
or just have some weights lying around,

00:49:20.940 --> 00:49:22.290
that's sufficient.

00:49:22.290 --> 00:49:25.420
- Okay. And all of this, the goal is...

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:27.570
It's not just we want to
look good, we want to...

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:31.850
All of this is aimed toward
the goal of longevity

00:49:31.850 --> 00:49:35.970
through putting your
longevity genes into...

00:49:35.970 --> 00:49:38.130
Or alerting your genes, your cells,

00:49:38.130 --> 00:49:39.740
to a state of adversity.
- Yeah.

00:49:39.740 --> 00:49:41.400
The adversity mimetics.

00:49:41.400 --> 00:49:42.260
- The adversity mimetics.

00:49:42.260 --> 00:49:43.240
I really do like this word.

00:49:43.240 --> 00:49:44.390
I think this is going to catch on.

00:49:44.390 --> 00:49:46.160
- Yeah, we should trademark that one, Rob.

00:49:46.160 --> 00:49:47.210
- Okay. [laughs]

00:49:47.210 --> 00:49:48.043
So let's...

00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:51.420
Exercise is not the
only adversity mimetic,

00:49:51.420 --> 00:49:53.400
there are others.

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:56.930
We're going to talk now
about a few other categories.

00:49:56.930 --> 00:49:58.550
We're going to alk
about cold temperatures.

00:49:58.550 --> 00:50:00.470
We're going to talk about heat.

00:50:00.470 --> 00:50:02.057
But there's a really
exciting one right now,

00:50:02.057 --> 00:50:03.690
and it is related, in some ways,

00:50:03.690 --> 00:50:05.300
to exercise or the effects...

00:50:05.300 --> 00:50:06.150
the genetic effects,

00:50:06.150 --> 00:50:09.310
the cellular effects that
we get from exercise,

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:12.030
and it's really fast moving.

00:50:12.030 --> 00:50:13.230
The latest studies were only out

00:50:13.230 --> 00:50:14.420
in the last couple of years,

00:50:14.420 --> 00:50:17.360
and this is hyperbaric oxygen treatment.

00:50:17.360 --> 00:50:19.350
- Right. Or HBO2.

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:20.630
Yeah, this is really fascinating

00:50:20.630 --> 00:50:25.010
because for a while we
knew that wounds repair

00:50:25.010 --> 00:50:27.420
or heal faster when you
give them more oxygen.

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:29.060
- Because why?

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:30.280
- Well, it's not clear.

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:33.380
It could be hormesis,
free radical generation.

00:50:33.380 --> 00:50:35.370
It could be that more
oxygen in the tissues

00:50:35.370 --> 00:50:36.763
allows them to grow better.

00:50:37.800 --> 00:50:38.820
Those are all theories.

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:40.330
But we know that it works.

00:50:40.330 --> 00:50:41.880
But then people started putting themselves

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:43.276
in hyperbaric chambers.

00:50:43.276 --> 00:50:45.420
The military, certainly the Navy,

00:50:45.420 --> 00:50:47.560
have been doing this for a long time

00:50:47.560 --> 00:50:49.660
to prevent the bends.

00:50:49.660 --> 00:50:51.730
But it's found to be quite therapeutic,

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:53.380
particularly for neurological disorders

00:50:53.380 --> 00:50:54.747
but increasingly for aging itself.

00:50:54.747 --> 00:50:57.033
- And we should say what a
hyperbaric chamber is, right?

00:50:57.033 --> 00:50:58.980
A hyperbaric chamber is a room

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:00.820
or sometimes just a little tube

00:51:00.820 --> 00:51:04.200
where the pressure is increased;

00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:05.720
and when the pressure is increased,

00:51:05.720 --> 00:51:08.270
the amount of oxygen that
you get when you breathe

00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:09.450
is increased.

00:51:09.450 --> 00:51:10.283
- Right.

00:51:10.283 --> 00:51:12.160
You can go up a couple
of atmospheres or more.

00:51:12.160 --> 00:51:14.400
And you supplement that with oxygen.

00:51:14.400 --> 00:51:18.000
You breathe in a bit more
oxygen or pure oxygen.

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:21.880
And so I recently tried this,
actually, I went out to L.A.

00:51:21.880 --> 00:51:24.620
Fortunately, unlike you,
I'm not claustrophobic.

00:51:24.620 --> 00:51:25.501
- I'm so...

00:51:25.501 --> 00:51:26.334
I couldn't do this.

00:51:26.334 --> 00:51:27.167
I can not do this.

00:51:27.167 --> 00:51:28.000
- You probably can't.

00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:29.960
But it's a glass cylinder,
so you don't feel it.

00:51:29.960 --> 00:51:31.710
And I just watched "Schitt's
Creek" for a while,

00:51:31.710 --> 00:51:33.757
it was pretty cool especially
when I was high on oxygen.

00:51:33.757 --> 00:51:34.930
- You said it was funnier when you...

00:51:34.930 --> 00:51:36.050
- It certainly was funnier,

00:51:36.050 --> 00:51:39.010
but it might just be because
every time I watch it

00:51:39.010 --> 00:51:40.360
it gets funnier.

00:51:40.360 --> 00:51:41.480
But it's really relaxing.

00:51:41.480 --> 00:51:43.960
I believe I was in
there for about an hour.

00:51:43.960 --> 00:51:46.730
And you can meditate in there

00:51:46.730 --> 00:51:48.810
or you can fall asleep or watch TV.

00:51:48.810 --> 00:51:50.110
It's really a great experience.

00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:51.400
But that aside,

00:51:51.400 --> 00:51:55.410
what the science says is
that it actually can reverse

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:56.870
an aspect of aging,

00:51:56.870 --> 00:51:58.210
which is telomere shortening.

00:51:58.210 --> 00:51:59.810
The ends of the chromosomes...

00:51:59.810 --> 00:52:02.400
Many of you will have heard of this.

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:05.450
Like the ends of the shoelaces,

00:52:05.450 --> 00:52:06.940
the aglets they're called;

00:52:06.940 --> 00:52:10.160
if they wear out, it actually
leads to cellular senescence,

00:52:10.160 --> 00:52:11.300
the zombie cells.

00:52:11.300 --> 00:52:13.410
And this happens over
time as we get older.

00:52:13.410 --> 00:52:15.120
And what we've been looking for,

00:52:15.120 --> 00:52:16.370
we scientists, for many years,

00:52:16.370 --> 00:52:18.010
is a molecule or a treatment

00:52:18.010 --> 00:52:19.770
that makes them grow back again

00:52:19.770 --> 00:52:21.640
'cause that should help slow down aging

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:22.960
and give us longer life.

00:52:22.960 --> 00:52:24.680
And that's what was reported last year

00:52:24.680 --> 00:52:26.640
from a group in Israel, who I know well.

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:29.670
I've been over there and
checked out their giant chamber.

00:52:29.670 --> 00:52:32.000
- And you actually got
wind of this before...

00:52:32.000 --> 00:52:33.390
while they were working on it,

00:52:33.390 --> 00:52:35.210
and you went to kind of like peek in

00:52:35.210 --> 00:52:36.043
on what they were doing.

00:52:36.043 --> 00:52:36.876
- Yeah.

00:52:36.876 --> 00:52:39.740
I mean, this is one of the
fortunate things about my job,

00:52:39.740 --> 00:52:42.770
is that I get to hear about
things before they're public

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:43.760
and I get a sneak preview.

00:52:43.760 --> 00:52:45.890
And I went over there and
sat in their giant chamber,

00:52:45.890 --> 00:52:48.390
which looks like the inside of a bus,

00:52:48.390 --> 00:52:50.170
and with a mask you put on,

00:52:50.170 --> 00:52:51.880
and they showed me their data.

00:52:51.880 --> 00:52:53.830
Actually it looked like a
control room from star Trek,

00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:55.550
it's really high-tech.

00:52:55.550 --> 00:52:57.750
But the data that they showed
me in mice and in humans

00:52:57.750 --> 00:52:58.710
was really compelling.

00:52:58.710 --> 00:53:00.960
It looked like, at least in the mice,

00:53:00.960 --> 00:53:04.150
that those plaques and
tangles in the brain of mice

00:53:04.150 --> 00:53:05.810
that were given Alzheimer's went away.

00:53:05.810 --> 00:53:07.780
- Do you have the little
miniature hyperbaric chambers

00:53:07.780 --> 00:53:08.690
for the mice?

00:53:08.690 --> 00:53:12.120
- We're building one 'cause
I'm going to use one in my lab,

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:15.090
but they're not ready yet.

00:53:15.090 --> 00:53:16.210
But also what they were finding

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:18.150
was that the humans that were put in there

00:53:18.150 --> 00:53:21.400
that had dementia or Parkinson's

00:53:21.400 --> 00:53:24.350
were dramatically improved
after a few sessions of this.

00:53:24.350 --> 00:53:27.330
And it's become a real
craze throughout the U.S.

00:53:27.330 --> 00:53:28.660
A lot of this is happening in L.A.

00:53:28.660 --> 00:53:30.523
where a lot of these trends start.

00:53:31.620 --> 00:53:33.470
People are reporting remarkable benefits.

00:53:33.470 --> 00:53:36.160
Now what we need are more
studies like the one in Israel

00:53:36.160 --> 00:53:38.750
to actually measure
things and, if possible,

00:53:38.750 --> 00:53:40.380
do a placebo control as well.

00:53:40.380 --> 00:53:43.950
- But at least what we have
right now are some studies,

00:53:43.950 --> 00:53:45.625
most of them fairly small-scale;

00:53:45.625 --> 00:53:47.480
but human studies,

00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:52.453
memory improvements,
telomeres, T helper cells.

00:53:53.390 --> 00:53:54.800
- Right. They go up as well.

00:53:54.800 --> 00:53:57.020
So in cognitive performance,

00:53:57.020 --> 00:53:58.250
there were those six elderly patients

00:53:58.250 --> 00:53:59.790
suffering from memory loss.

00:53:59.790 --> 00:54:02.840
And in 2021, this new study
came out and they all improved.

00:54:02.840 --> 00:54:06.030
- And the really cool
thing about this treatment,

00:54:06.030 --> 00:54:07.440
even though, look, like,

00:54:07.440 --> 00:54:10.270
not everybody has access to a
hyperbaric chamber, obviously,

00:54:10.270 --> 00:54:14.760
but it is a pretty easy intervention.

00:54:14.760 --> 00:54:17.640
And it's easy to treat people this way,

00:54:17.640 --> 00:54:20.809
it's easy to test people in this way.

00:54:20.809 --> 00:54:21.900
So if it it works-
- And now we have the clocks.

00:54:21.900 --> 00:54:22.733
We have the clocks.

00:54:22.733 --> 00:54:25.070
We can test if aging
actually goes backwards.

00:54:25.070 --> 00:54:26.090
That would be a good experiment

00:54:26.090 --> 00:54:29.320
is to take people who have done 7 or 8,

00:54:29.320 --> 00:54:32.430
10 bouts of this
treatment before and after

00:54:32.430 --> 00:54:35.320
and see if their biological
age went backwards.

00:54:35.320 --> 00:54:36.510
- So it's still early.

00:54:36.510 --> 00:54:38.440
It seems like these things are working

00:54:38.440 --> 00:54:39.970
across a variety of ways

00:54:39.970 --> 00:54:43.130
that we can measure aging
and the problems of aging.

00:54:43.130 --> 00:54:45.560
If this is working, why is it working?

00:54:45.560 --> 00:54:46.530
- Yeah.

00:54:46.530 --> 00:54:47.880
Well, we don't know for sure.

00:54:47.880 --> 00:54:49.630
But one of the theories

00:54:49.630 --> 00:54:52.090
is that it's working similar to exercise.

00:54:52.090 --> 00:54:55.090
So hypoxia, the low oxygen
when you go for a run,

00:54:55.090 --> 00:54:58.530
what it's doing is turning
on this HIF-1alpha protein

00:54:58.530 --> 00:55:00.170
that I talked about earlier,

00:55:00.170 --> 00:55:04.820
and that helps promote health in the body.

00:55:04.820 --> 00:55:06.720
The other thing that happens with exercise

00:55:06.720 --> 00:55:08.120
is the free radical generation,

00:55:08.120 --> 00:55:10.850
that gives you a little
bit of mitohormesis.

00:55:10.850 --> 00:55:13.573
It looks very much like hyperoxia.

00:55:14.690 --> 00:55:17.530
Hyperbaric chamber treatment
does the very similar thing.

00:55:17.530 --> 00:55:20.520
You get a production
of these free radicals

00:55:20.520 --> 00:55:23.430
and that stimulates also
a mitohormesis response

00:55:23.430 --> 00:55:26.270
that gives you very similar
benefits to low oxygen.

00:55:26.270 --> 00:55:28.200
There's one theory that I have,

00:55:28.200 --> 00:55:31.600
is that when you come down
from the high levels of oxygen,

00:55:31.600 --> 00:55:35.700
it simulates hypoxia like you're running,

00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:38.180
as you come down from
high levels to low levels.

00:55:38.180 --> 00:55:40.140
- So it's not so much the oxygen,

00:55:40.140 --> 00:55:42.750
the explosion of oxygen
that you're getting,

00:55:42.750 --> 00:55:45.610
but it's like once you like
temporarily get used to that

00:55:45.610 --> 00:55:47.303
then you come off of it-

00:55:47.303 --> 00:55:48.730
- It's the differential.

00:55:48.730 --> 00:55:50.660
And that fits with the new findings,

00:55:50.660 --> 00:55:53.900
which is going up once
and coming down once

00:55:53.900 --> 00:55:56.040
is not as good as going up
and down and up and down

00:55:56.040 --> 00:55:57.230
within a treatment.

00:55:57.230 --> 00:56:02.230
- Before we move away from
hyperbaric oxygen treatment,

00:56:02.590 --> 00:56:04.780
I know you're most familiar
with this Israeli study.

00:56:04.780 --> 00:56:06.790
Let's talk about the protocol in that one.

00:56:06.790 --> 00:56:07.990
- Yeah, they took 30 people

00:56:07.990 --> 00:56:10.250
and put them in their large
chamber, the one that I visited.

00:56:10.250 --> 00:56:14.200
And they did 60 daily
sessions of 90 minutes,

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:15.680
five of those per week,

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:17.520
and had a look at the
number of senescent cells

00:56:17.520 --> 00:56:20.400
in the bloodstream before and
after and telomere length.

00:56:20.400 --> 00:56:22.100
And what they found remarkably

00:56:22.100 --> 00:56:24.930
was that those aspects
of aging were reversed.

00:56:24.930 --> 00:56:27.410
- They saw impacts in senescent cell too?

00:56:27.410 --> 00:56:28.300
- They did.

00:56:28.300 --> 00:56:29.525
In the immune system,

00:56:29.525 --> 00:56:31.250
looking at these immune cells,

00:56:31.250 --> 00:56:32.860
you can measure senescence.

00:56:32.860 --> 00:56:34.810
You can stain them blue,

00:56:34.810 --> 00:56:37.330
which is bright blue when you get older,

00:56:37.330 --> 00:56:39.430
and they found that there
was a dramatic reduction

00:56:39.430 --> 00:56:42.370
in the number of those
senescent cytotoxic T-cells,

00:56:42.370 --> 00:56:43.590
as they're called.

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:45.930
- Okay, so 60 sessions, 90 minutes,

00:56:45.930 --> 00:56:50.000
this is not something most
people can do every day

00:56:50.000 --> 00:56:50.833
right now.

00:56:50.833 --> 00:56:53.150
This is not going to be a common part

00:56:53.150 --> 00:56:57.430
of people's longevity
regimens anytime soon.

00:56:57.430 --> 00:56:59.270
- Well, there are a number of centers

00:56:59.270 --> 00:57:01.720
that are run by doctors in major cities.

00:57:01.720 --> 00:57:04.660
It will become more popular,
it's growing rapidly.

00:57:04.660 --> 00:57:06.700
But I think it's a great way in the future

00:57:06.700 --> 00:57:09.126
for people to mimic exercise.

00:57:09.126 --> 00:57:11.400
I wouldn't say don't exercise, right?

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:13.040
But it's an interesting idea

00:57:13.040 --> 00:57:15.020
that you can lie down, watch TV,

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:17.738
go to sleep and get your running

00:57:17.738 --> 00:57:20.150
by mimicking hypoxia,

00:57:20.150 --> 00:57:22.740
but in this case with high
levels of oxygen in your body

00:57:22.740 --> 00:57:23.593
for a short period of time.

00:57:23.593 --> 00:57:24.880
- It's really cool.

00:57:24.880 --> 00:57:29.680
Let's shift now to talking
about cold therapy,

00:57:29.680 --> 00:57:32.370
which is another thing
that a lot of people

00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:33.390
are experimenting with now,

00:57:33.390 --> 00:57:37.270
another thing that there are
centers for all over the place,

00:57:37.270 --> 00:57:39.380
but that the research is
still sort of catching up

00:57:39.380 --> 00:57:40.353
to the excitement.

00:57:42.030 --> 00:57:46.630
So we're sort of on the edge
here of our understanding.

00:57:46.630 --> 00:57:50.030
But we do know some things
about what cold does

00:57:50.030 --> 00:57:50.870
to the human body

00:57:50.870 --> 00:57:53.960
and actually what cold does to organisms

00:57:53.960 --> 00:57:55.730
across the spectrum.
- Yeah.

00:57:55.730 --> 00:57:57.100
In fact, when we wrote "Lifespan,"

00:57:57.100 --> 00:57:59.000
there was almost no data on this.

00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:01.480
But now we know actually
that one of the huge benefits

00:58:01.480 --> 00:58:05.370
you get from being cold is
the production of brown fat.

00:58:05.370 --> 00:58:06.530
So what is brown fat?

00:58:06.530 --> 00:58:11.530
Brown, or it's often called
beige fat, is found in babies.

00:58:11.670 --> 00:58:13.620
It's typically to allow them to stay warm

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:14.550
because they don't shiver

00:58:14.550 --> 00:58:17.470
until they're about one year of age.

00:58:17.470 --> 00:58:20.605
And what was discovered
about 10 years ago at Harvard

00:58:20.605 --> 00:58:23.800
by Bruce Spiegelman and Ron
Kahn, a couple of my colleagues,

00:58:23.800 --> 00:58:26.680
is that adults also have some brown fat,

00:58:26.680 --> 00:58:29.270
and they discovered
this with PET scanning,

00:58:29.270 --> 00:58:32.100
and they found it mostly
exists on your back

00:58:32.100 --> 00:58:33.330
in your shoulder blades.

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:36.050
And when you get cold, it revs up,

00:58:36.050 --> 00:58:37.990
you get more of this brown fat.

00:58:37.990 --> 00:58:40.310
And this is a good thing because brown fat

00:58:40.310 --> 00:58:41.700
is extremely healthy.

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:46.010
It revs up metabolism, it burns white fat.

00:58:46.010 --> 00:58:49.120
And we think that there are
these factors, little chemicals,

00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:52.030
little proteins that get
secreted out of brown fat

00:58:52.030 --> 00:58:54.340
that make the rest of
the body healthy as well.

00:58:54.340 --> 00:58:55.420
One of the reasons we know that

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:58.210
is because there's a gene
that makes brown fat,

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:01.210
makes cells turn brown, from
white to beige to brown,

00:59:01.210 --> 00:59:03.660
and it's called PRDM16.

00:59:03.660 --> 00:59:06.030
And mice that lack this gene,

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:07.070
they don't have brown fat

00:59:07.070 --> 00:59:08.950
but they also develop type two diabetes

00:59:08.950 --> 00:59:10.993
and cardiovascular disease as a result.

00:59:11.870 --> 00:59:16.870
- Cold impacts not just humans,
it impacts all organisms.

00:59:17.200 --> 00:59:18.730
We've seen this in model organisms too,

00:59:18.730 --> 00:59:19.563
these effects, right?

00:59:19.563 --> 00:59:20.890
There's studies in worms,

00:59:20.890 --> 00:59:22.323
there's studies in mice.

00:59:23.230 --> 00:59:24.840
You've got a couple of favorites, I know.

00:59:24.840 --> 00:59:26.027
You like talking about the mice

00:59:26.027 --> 00:59:27.600
that got to spoon together.

00:59:27.600 --> 00:59:28.433
- Oh yeah.

00:59:28.433 --> 00:59:32.000
So that one is a bit of a
disappointment in the field.

00:59:32.000 --> 00:59:34.870
We had these dwarf mice
that Mike Bonkowski,

00:59:34.870 --> 00:59:36.770
who was working in my lab,

00:59:36.770 --> 00:59:39.250
he was the guy that generated
the longest lived mouse,

00:59:39.250 --> 00:59:40.310
he called it Yoda.

00:59:40.310 --> 00:59:43.070
And it was very long lived
because it had a mutation

00:59:43.070 --> 00:59:45.720
in a gene for the growth hormone receptor,

00:59:45.720 --> 00:59:47.810
which means they were small and dwarfs.

00:59:47.810 --> 00:59:50.920
And these little small
dwarf mice would live

00:59:50.920 --> 00:59:52.900
up to three times longer
than normal mouse,

00:59:52.900 --> 00:59:54.560
it was quite an amazing thing.

00:59:54.560 --> 00:59:55.807
And the field was rejoicing:

00:59:55.807 --> 00:59:57.790
"Wow, we've figured
out how to make mammals

00:59:57.790 --> 00:59:59.130
live that much longer."

00:59:59.130 --> 01:00:01.670
And then it was noticed
that they were shivering,

01:00:01.670 --> 01:00:02.537
little cold mice.

01:00:02.537 --> 01:00:04.587
And so the researchers thought,

01:00:04.587 --> 01:00:06.310
"We'll just give them friends."

01:00:06.310 --> 01:00:08.240
- They weren't shivering
because the lab was cold,

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:10.090
they were shivering because?

01:00:10.090 --> 01:00:11.320
- They didn't have a companion.

01:00:11.320 --> 01:00:14.870
Mice like to live not
solitary, but with other mice.

01:00:14.870 --> 01:00:16.460
And so they gave them a buddy,

01:00:16.460 --> 01:00:18.220
Each of these dwarves had a buddy.

01:00:18.220 --> 01:00:21.750
And then the majority of the
lifespan extension went away.

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:22.680
- When they got their buddy?

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:23.513
- Yeah.

01:00:23.513 --> 01:00:24.950
Which is super disappointing, right?

01:00:24.950 --> 01:00:28.880
But what it told us was that
a large effect of longevity

01:00:28.880 --> 01:00:30.820
was due to them being cold.

01:00:30.820 --> 01:00:32.140
- And why is that happening?

01:00:32.140 --> 01:00:34.590
When we go back to thinking
about the survival circuit,

01:00:34.590 --> 01:00:35.740
what's happening?

01:00:35.740 --> 01:00:38.250
- Yeah, well, the trick is
let's go back to the brown fat.

01:00:38.250 --> 01:00:41.310
What brown fat has a
lot of is mitochondria.

01:00:41.310 --> 01:00:42.630
And within those mitochondria,

01:00:42.630 --> 01:00:44.450
they're actually quite different.

01:00:44.450 --> 01:00:47.190
They have high levels
of proteins called UCPs,

01:00:47.190 --> 01:00:48.720
or uncoupling proteins,

01:00:48.720 --> 01:00:51.440
which insert into the
membrane of the mitochondria

01:00:51.440 --> 01:00:55.380
and allow those protons that
were built up to leak through;

01:00:55.380 --> 01:00:58.260
instead of going through that
pump that makes the energy,

01:00:58.260 --> 01:00:59.920
they leak through.

01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:00.990
Why is that interesting?

01:01:00.990 --> 01:01:01.823
Well, first of all,

01:01:01.823 --> 01:01:03.810
that generates heat that helps the animal

01:01:03.810 --> 01:01:05.690
and we survive cold,

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:08.060
but also you get fewer
free radicals produced

01:01:08.060 --> 01:01:10.580
when you uncouple mitochondria
with these proteins.

01:01:10.580 --> 01:01:13.130
And consistent with that,

01:01:13.130 --> 01:01:14.240
if you make an animal,

01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:17.670
let's say it's a a worm
or a fly or even a mouse,

01:01:17.670 --> 01:01:20.920
that has high levels of these UCP genes,

01:01:20.920 --> 01:01:22.520
they actually live longer.

01:01:22.520 --> 01:01:26.520
So uncoupling and reducing
that free radical load;

01:01:26.520 --> 01:01:28.730
and you can do this with
cold therapy, we think;

01:01:28.730 --> 01:01:30.670
is beneficial to health.

01:01:30.670 --> 01:01:34.263
- And cold therapy, people
call it cryotherapy,

01:01:36.280 --> 01:01:39.640
is this like short-term
acute, super-intense...

01:01:39.640 --> 01:01:40.880
You've done this before, right?

01:01:40.880 --> 01:01:42.082
You did this with Rogan, didn't you?

01:01:42.082 --> 01:01:42.915
- Yeah.

01:01:42.915 --> 01:01:45.800
Joe said let's go do that
after the show, which I did.

01:01:45.800 --> 01:01:46.633
It was pretty chill.

01:01:46.633 --> 01:01:49.010
I had to strip down to my underwear

01:01:49.010 --> 01:01:51.080
and put gloves on and socks and whatever.

01:01:51.080 --> 01:01:54.680
But you get to play music, at least.

01:01:54.680 --> 01:01:56.180
I got to choose "Help!" by the Beatles,

01:01:56.180 --> 01:01:57.220
which was quite appropriate

01:01:57.220 --> 01:01:59.360
because by the end of those three minutes-

01:01:59.360 --> 01:02:00.660
- You wanted out.

01:02:00.660 --> 01:02:01.887
- Well, the first two minutes, I'm like,

01:02:01.887 --> 01:02:03.160
"What's the problem? This isn't cold."

01:02:03.160 --> 01:02:04.890
But that last minute,

01:02:04.890 --> 01:02:07.060
I really thought I was
going to get hypothermia

01:02:07.060 --> 01:02:08.310
and my ears might break off.

01:02:08.310 --> 01:02:09.480
- You're chattering and shaking-

01:02:09.480 --> 01:02:11.830
- Yeah, you really start to shiver,

01:02:11.830 --> 01:02:13.650
which is your body's
way of generating heat

01:02:13.650 --> 01:02:15.240
by moving your muscles.

01:02:15.240 --> 01:02:17.000
But it was really enjoyable.

01:02:17.000 --> 01:02:19.500
I found afterwards I felt stimulated.

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:21.360
I felt lucky to be alive.

01:02:21.360 --> 01:02:23.650
And I felt good for a
number of days afterwards,

01:02:23.650 --> 01:02:25.910
which is probably because my
mitochondria were revved up

01:02:25.910 --> 01:02:27.580
and I was building more brown fat.

01:02:27.580 --> 01:02:29.140
The one thing I think
it's worth pausing here

01:02:29.140 --> 01:02:30.310
to talk about is:

01:02:30.310 --> 01:02:31.710
Why would you do this now?

01:02:31.710 --> 01:02:34.460
Why don't you wait till
you're old to do cold therapy?

01:02:34.460 --> 01:02:35.820
And what's been found, at least in mice,

01:02:35.820 --> 01:02:40.140
is that old mice don't make
brown fat as well as young mice.

01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:42.040
So what you want to do is, middle-age,

01:02:42.040 --> 01:02:43.453
do these treatments

01:02:43.453 --> 01:02:46.740
so that you're ready for old
age when it becomes harder.

01:02:46.740 --> 01:02:49.780
- Just like right now we need
to be doing vigorous exercise.

01:02:49.780 --> 01:02:52.360
We can't wait until we're
old to do vigorous exercise.

01:02:52.360 --> 01:02:53.660
- Yeah, though it's never too late.

01:02:53.660 --> 01:02:55.860
We do find that things
work in elderly mice

01:02:55.860 --> 01:02:57.000
and elderly people,

01:02:57.000 --> 01:02:59.550
but it doesn't work as
well if you start midlife

01:02:59.550 --> 01:03:00.470
or even earlier.

01:03:00.470 --> 01:03:01.303
- Okay.

01:03:01.303 --> 01:03:03.130
And maybe that's like
the cryotherapy stuff

01:03:03.130 --> 01:03:04.890
is sort of a can-do vigorous exercise,

01:03:04.890 --> 01:03:06.997
but you still have to have that low level

01:03:06.997 --> 01:03:08.850
of perceived adversity

01:03:08.850 --> 01:03:10.240
just like we have to get lots

01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:12.163
and lots of steps in every day.

01:03:13.060 --> 01:03:15.030
But we live in these environments,

01:03:15.030 --> 01:03:16.470
these temperature controlled environments

01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:18.940
where it's like 68 to 72.

01:03:18.940 --> 01:03:21.050
That's not how our ancestors lived at all.

01:03:21.050 --> 01:03:22.770
- No, we were shivering
probably most of the time

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:24.140
during the ice age for sure,

01:03:24.140 --> 01:03:26.100
and our bodies recognize that.

01:03:26.100 --> 01:03:27.470
We've got those genes that respond

01:03:27.470 --> 01:03:30.060
and they keep us healthy,
as we've talked about.

01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:31.130
So there are a number of ways you can...

01:03:31.130 --> 01:03:32.980
If you don't have a
cryotherapy center near you,

01:03:32.980 --> 01:03:34.100
what do you do?

01:03:34.100 --> 01:03:35.280
Well, you can take cold showers.

01:03:35.280 --> 01:03:36.113
Some people do that.

01:03:36.113 --> 01:03:37.340
I don't, I find that unpleasant.

01:03:37.340 --> 01:03:40.380
But you can do that, it's
very cheap to do that.

01:03:40.380 --> 01:03:42.650
Or you can do something
that I do actually do,

01:03:42.650 --> 01:03:45.070
which is sleep with very
few covers on my bed

01:03:45.070 --> 01:03:46.510
and lower down my body temperature.

01:03:46.510 --> 01:03:48.070
And that's also been shown to activate

01:03:48.070 --> 01:03:51.130
these uncoupling proteins
and build brown fat.

01:03:51.130 --> 01:03:54.970
- The opposite side of this is heat,

01:03:54.970 --> 01:03:55.930
which is also something

01:03:55.930 --> 01:03:57.340
that throughout our evolutionary history

01:03:57.340 --> 01:03:58.173
we've had to deal with.

01:03:58.173 --> 01:04:00.760
We didn't always live in
temperature controlled buildings.

01:04:00.760 --> 01:04:04.830
And we've got some emerging
research on that as well.

01:04:04.830 --> 01:04:07.040
- Right, taking your body
out of its comfort zone.

01:04:07.040 --> 01:04:08.080
And so-

01:04:08.080 --> 01:04:09.740
- All of these things are
about taking your body

01:04:09.740 --> 01:04:10.573
out of it's comfort zone, right?

01:04:10.573 --> 01:04:11.406
- They are, they are.

01:04:11.406 --> 01:04:12.500
And some of them are really enjoyable.

01:04:12.500 --> 01:04:14.980
I think sauna bathing,
as it's called in Europe,

01:04:14.980 --> 01:04:16.600
is super enjoyable.

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:17.880
And it also is good for your skin,

01:04:17.880 --> 01:04:20.910
you get to sweat and get
those pores unclogged.

01:04:20.910 --> 01:04:24.830
But this is one of the most
ancient therapies for longevity.

01:04:24.830 --> 01:04:26.010
Even before Roman times,

01:04:26.010 --> 01:04:28.480
they were bathing in these saunas,

01:04:28.480 --> 01:04:31.670
though the Romans would
use fires under the floor.

01:04:31.670 --> 01:04:33.920
But this is something that
European still carry on

01:04:33.920 --> 01:04:34.753
as a tradition,

01:04:34.753 --> 01:04:37.240
particularly in Finland and
other Scandinavian countries,

01:04:37.240 --> 01:04:39.980
where these studies are
typically performed on men

01:04:39.980 --> 01:04:40.813
for some reason.

01:04:40.813 --> 01:04:42.640
But the data that I've looked at,

01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:45.030
which we'll put in the show notes,

01:04:45.030 --> 01:04:47.400
is that there's absolutely no doubt

01:04:47.400 --> 01:04:52.020
that men who partake in sauna
bathing a few times a week,

01:04:52.020 --> 01:04:55.050
often at home 'cause they
build this into their houses,

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:57.470
have a dramatic reduction, up to 20%,

01:04:57.470 --> 01:04:59.660
in the rate of cardiovascular disease

01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:01.720
and mortality caused by heart attacks.

01:05:01.720 --> 01:05:05.183
- This isn't happening for the
same reason as cold therapy.

01:05:05.183 --> 01:05:06.610
This has nothing to do with brown fat.

01:05:06.610 --> 01:05:08.100
There's another thing
that's going on here, right?

01:05:08.100 --> 01:05:09.060
- It's different in this case.

01:05:09.060 --> 01:05:10.500
What we think goes on in a sauna

01:05:10.500 --> 01:05:12.580
is you're activating HSPs.

01:05:12.580 --> 01:05:14.830
And these heat shock proteins are helping

01:05:14.830 --> 01:05:17.460
to fold proteins correctly

01:05:17.460 --> 01:05:20.140
and also stimulate pathways
that are beneficial,

01:05:20.140 --> 01:05:22.010
such as building new blood vessels,

01:05:22.010 --> 01:05:23.620
making more mitochondria.

01:05:23.620 --> 01:05:26.620
And one of the reasons
that I believe it's true

01:05:26.620 --> 01:05:29.940
is that in model organisms, take a worm,

01:05:29.940 --> 01:05:32.255
if you turn up his shock proteins,

01:05:32.255 --> 01:05:34.170
either by giving them a lot of heat

01:05:34.170 --> 01:05:35.920
or genetically modifying them,

01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:37.073
they also live longer.

01:05:38.230 --> 01:05:41.630
- We don't quite know what dosing is right

01:05:41.630 --> 01:05:44.100
for sauna baths at this point,

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:46.320
or like how much, how hot the...

01:05:46.320 --> 01:05:48.523
Not a ton of research in this area.

01:05:49.390 --> 01:05:51.290
- No, but it doesn't seem
like you can overdo it.

01:05:51.290 --> 01:05:53.803
I haven't seen any evidence
that it's negative.

01:05:53.803 --> 01:05:55.400
And I used to...

01:05:55.400 --> 01:05:56.540
Before the pandemic,

01:05:56.540 --> 01:05:58.450
I used to go at least once a week

01:05:58.450 --> 01:06:02.440
and do multiple bouts of
the heat shock in the sauna

01:06:02.440 --> 01:06:03.610
for about 15 minutes

01:06:03.610 --> 01:06:06.140
and then jump in an ice
bath which was nearby

01:06:06.140 --> 01:06:06.973
for four minutes,

01:06:06.973 --> 01:06:07.947
and then cycle that.

01:06:07.947 --> 01:06:09.510
- You should probably consult your doctor

01:06:09.510 --> 01:06:10.430
before you do that.

01:06:10.430 --> 01:06:12.440
- I would, definitely.

01:06:12.440 --> 01:06:13.890
And I did.

01:06:13.890 --> 01:06:15.950
But, yeah, it can put
some stress on the body,

01:06:15.950 --> 01:06:17.130
certainly on the heart.

01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:20.360
But, yeah, the idea though
is to shock the body;

01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:21.880
heat, cold, heat, cold.

01:06:21.880 --> 01:06:23.610
And that way, I think,

01:06:23.610 --> 01:06:27.230
you get the maximum benefit
from these adversity mimetics.

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:29.410
- And again, you don't
necessarily need to have access

01:06:29.410 --> 01:06:30.430
to a sauna to do this.

01:06:30.430 --> 01:06:31.940
I mean, some people can
build them into their home,

01:06:31.940 --> 01:06:33.210
some people have them in their gyms,

01:06:33.210 --> 01:06:35.180
that's kind of a point of privilege.

01:06:35.180 --> 01:06:37.430
Most people have a bath or shower.

01:06:37.430 --> 01:06:38.263
- Or hot shower.

01:06:38.263 --> 01:06:39.490
I certainly do that.

01:06:39.490 --> 01:06:40.980
I like to turn the heat way up.

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:42.520
There are a variety of different saunas.

01:06:42.520 --> 01:06:43.670
There's the old fashioned type,

01:06:43.670 --> 01:06:45.430
which is the cedar planks,

01:06:45.430 --> 01:06:48.500
sitting in there and you just
throw water on hot rocks;

01:06:48.500 --> 01:06:49.333
that's traditional.

01:06:49.333 --> 01:06:51.850
There are new ones such
as infrared saunas,

01:06:51.850 --> 01:06:53.820
and the infrared light
actually penetrate the skin

01:06:53.820 --> 01:06:56.170
and is thought, and there's some evidence;

01:06:56.170 --> 01:06:57.670
real, believable evidence;

01:06:57.670 --> 01:07:00.370
that they also, in the skin layer,

01:07:00.370 --> 01:07:02.030
can reverse aspects of aging as well,

01:07:02.030 --> 01:07:04.663
and including improve hair growth.

01:07:06.110 --> 01:07:08.030
- For some obvious reasons,

01:07:08.030 --> 01:07:10.670
there's a lot of questions I
want to ask about hair growth.

01:07:10.670 --> 01:07:13.830
We're going to be discussing
hair growth, skin care,

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:17.000
all these like sort of like
cosmetic aspects of aging things

01:07:17.000 --> 01:07:18.600
in a future episode.

01:07:18.600 --> 01:07:21.420
One of the commonalities
that I'm kind of sensing

01:07:21.420 --> 01:07:22.740
in all of these things, right,

01:07:22.740 --> 01:07:24.387
you do vigorous exercise

01:07:24.387 --> 01:07:26.800
and you get what we term a runner's high,

01:07:26.800 --> 01:07:29.310
and you talked about doing the cryotherapy

01:07:29.310 --> 01:07:30.610
and you felt like...

01:07:30.610 --> 01:07:31.443
What did you say?

01:07:31.443 --> 01:07:32.763
Like, blessed to be alive.

01:07:33.870 --> 01:07:38.270
I've done sweats and sweat lodges,

01:07:38.270 --> 01:07:39.103
and you come out of those things

01:07:39.103 --> 01:07:42.653
and it's just like it's
the best drug in the world.

01:07:43.970 --> 01:07:45.222
What do you think...

01:07:45.222 --> 01:07:47.120
Why are these things so similar?

01:07:47.120 --> 01:07:48.780
This happens in the
hyperbaric chamber too.

01:07:48.780 --> 01:07:51.180
- Yeah, that's one of
the nice side effects.

01:07:51.180 --> 01:07:53.390
That's when you know that you're doing

01:07:53.390 --> 01:07:54.660
a good longevity protocol.

01:07:54.660 --> 01:07:56.460
'Cause what's happening
at the cellular level

01:07:56.460 --> 01:07:59.860
is those three main defense
pathways get turned on;

01:07:59.860 --> 01:08:03.140
but at the physiological
level, the neurological level,

01:08:03.140 --> 01:08:04.970
you're getting endorphins as well,

01:08:04.970 --> 01:08:05.803
and that's when you know

01:08:05.803 --> 01:08:07.330
you've actually done the right thing.

01:08:07.330 --> 01:08:11.257
- So let's talk about a basic protocol

01:08:11.257 --> 01:08:13.070
with the caveat that we always make,

01:08:13.070 --> 01:08:16.440
which is everybody's going
to be a little bit different.

01:08:16.440 --> 01:08:21.440
But if we're trying to
get sort of like exercise,

01:08:21.850 --> 01:08:25.233
cold, heat and hyperbaric,

01:08:27.467 --> 01:08:29.690
what's a good way to do that?

01:08:29.690 --> 01:08:31.320
We've already talked about
the exercise protocol.

01:08:31.320 --> 01:08:33.097
Actually, I don't think
